1
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:18,655
Q-u-a-r...

2
00:01:22,032 --> 00:01:23,632
a-n-t...

3
00:01:26,579 --> 00:01:28,346
i-n-e.

4
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:34,660
Quarantine.

5
00:01:35,427 --> 00:01:37,586
Q-u-a-r...

6
00:01:41,552 --> 00:01:43,426
a-n-t...

7
00:01:44,770 --> 00:01:46,417
i-n-e.

8
00:01:47,898 --> 00:01:49,507
Quarantine.

9
00:01:49,611 --> 00:01:52,926
- You know the cholera?
- Yes, Mother.

10
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:56,135
You've seen the signs on the houses
where the coloureds live?

11
00:01:56,240 --> 00:01:57,932
Yes, Mother.

12
00:01:58,036 --> 00:02:00,944
- You know the typhus?
- I do, Mother.

13
00:02:04,492 --> 00:02:07,233
You know what they can do to you?

14
00:02:07,337 --> 00:02:09,700
Yes, Mother.

15
00:02:12,708 --> 00:02:16,108
You are not safe.

16
00:02:22,176 --> 00:02:25,789
You may be directing this movie, Howard,
but what you're asking, we can't do it.

17
00:02:25,893 --> 00:02:28,668
Don't tell me I can't do it.
Don't tell me it can't be done.

18
00:02:28,772 --> 00:02:30,880
The gyro forces are too much here.

19
00:02:30,985 --> 00:02:32,655
You send these planes into simultaneous...

20
00:02:32,759 --> 00:02:36,465
It's the goddamn climax of the picture,
Frank, all right? You make it work.

21
00:02:36,569 --> 00:02:38,849
Look, decrease the vertical trajectory,
if you have to.

22
00:02:38,953 --> 00:02:41,723
A LeRhone rotary won't stall at 60 degrees,
I've done it.

23
00:02:41,827 --> 00:02:45,599
Howard, we're still short two cameras.
We need to cut that sequence down...

24
00:02:45,704 --> 00:02:48,087
We're not cutting anything, all right.
I'll get those extra cameras.

25
00:02:48,191 --> 00:02:48,984
Set up for rehearsals in five.

26
00:02:49,088 --> 00:02:51,820
Mr Hughes, I'm Noah Dietrich.
Your office said I might find you here.

27
00:02:51,924 --> 00:02:56,739
Dietrich. You're a man on the come. I read
your résumé and talked to your references.

28
00:02:56,843 --> 00:02:58,489
You know what I'm looking for?

29
00:02:58,594 --> 00:03:01,663
As I understand it, you're looking for
a second in command at Hughes Tools.

30
00:03:01,768 --> 00:03:04,579
Someone to help oversee
the financial aspects of the business.

31
00:03:04,683 --> 00:03:07,457
Yeah. I'm looking for somebody
to run it and do a damn good job.

32
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:09,835
Look, there's really one thing
you've got to know.

33
00:03:09,939 --> 00:03:12,962
My folks, they're gone now,
so it's my money.

34
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:15,302
Now, what I choose to do with
that money may seem crazy

35
00:03:15,406 --> 00:03:18,117
to those sons of bitches in Houston -
and I'm sure as hell it does -

36
00:03:18,222 --> 00:03:20,512
but it all makes good sense to me.
You got that?

37
00:03:20,617 --> 00:03:22,512
- Got it.
- Good.

38
00:03:22,617 --> 00:03:25,808
Now, you made, what,
$5,200 a year on your last job?

39
00:03:25,913 --> 00:03:28,270
I'll pay you 10,000.

40
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:31,443
- Guess I'll be working twice as hard.
- You'll be working four times as hard.

41
00:03:31,547 --> 00:03:35,110
I just got you at half-price, Mr Dietrich.
Welcome aboard.

42
00:03:35,215 --> 00:03:37,053
You're my voice now.
Make 'em understand that.

43
00:03:37,157 --> 00:03:39,743
Look, some of those fine folks down there
still call me "Junior".

44
00:03:39,847 --> 00:03:42,992
- You tell them it's "Mr Hughes" now.
- You bet.

45
00:03:43,096 --> 00:03:46,747
- So, when do we go to Houston?
- We don't. Cholera epidemic in 1913.

46
00:03:46,851 --> 00:03:49,624
2,000 dead. Whole place is
nothing but pestilential swamp.

47
00:03:49,728 --> 00:03:54,045
Typhus, malaria, cholera, yellow fever -
you name it, they got it.

48
00:03:54,149 --> 00:03:56,545
You see that, Mr Dietrich?

49
00:03:56,650 --> 00:04:00,885
You are looking at the largest
private air force in the entire world.

50
00:04:00,989 --> 00:04:05,360
- What do you think of that, now?
- It's your money.

51
00:04:05,905 --> 00:04:08,505
Start 'em up.

52
00:05:34,535 --> 00:05:36,937
I just saw the rough cut of
the new Tod Browning movie,

53
00:05:37,042 --> 00:05:40,445
<i>London After Midnight.</i>
Lon Chaney is incredible.

54
00:05:40,549 --> 00:05:45,250
It's his best performance in a long time. It's
gonna be a big hit. The cards are great.

55
00:05:47,047 --> 00:05:49,495
Hello, Mr Mayer.

56
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:52,705
I don't know if you remember me.
My name's Howard Hughes.

57
00:05:52,809 --> 00:05:55,167
I was wondering
if I could have a moment of your time.

58
00:05:55,271 --> 00:05:57,878
Yeah, Howard Hughes.
The airplane picture, right?

59
00:05:57,982 --> 00:05:59,423
- Exactly.
- Yeah, I remember.

60
00:05:59,528 --> 00:06:02,260
- <i>Hell's Angels.</i> You heard of it. Good.
- Yes.

61
00:06:02,364 --> 00:06:04,930
Listen, I was wondering
if I could have a moment of your time.

62
00:06:05,034 --> 00:06:07,510
I need a few cameras.

63
00:06:07,614 --> 00:06:09,308
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

64
00:06:09,413 --> 00:06:11,306
Two, to be exact.

65
00:06:11,410 --> 00:06:13,146
I already bought every camera I could find,

66
00:06:13,251 --> 00:06:15,336
but we're shooting
our big dogfight sequence this week

67
00:06:15,440 --> 00:06:19,104
and I need two more, desperately.
Do you think MGM could help me out?

68
00:06:19,209 --> 00:06:21,819
- With what?
- Cameras.

69
00:06:21,923 --> 00:06:26,874
With the actual cam... We're not usually in
the practice of helping out the competition.

70
00:06:28,047 --> 00:06:31,879
- How many cameras do you have now?
- 24.

71
00:06:35,471 --> 00:06:37,796
Wait a minute.

72
00:06:39,350 --> 00:06:41,003
- You have 24 cameras?
- That's right.

73
00:06:41,108 --> 00:06:44,010
- And you need two more?
- Yeah.

74
00:06:44,114 --> 00:06:47,717
- You don't think you got it with 24?
- No. No, sir.

75
00:06:47,822 --> 00:06:50,475
You know, I think we've got them all...

76
00:06:50,580 --> 00:06:53,055
Don't we have them all?
They're all used.

77
00:06:53,159 --> 00:06:55,519
- All 26 of them.
- I only need two, sir.

78
00:06:55,623 --> 00:06:57,647
- Jesus Christ, sonny...
- Howard.

79
00:06:57,751 --> 00:07:00,606
Howard.
Let me give you a little advice.

80
00:07:00,711 --> 00:07:03,107
- Why don't you take your oil money...
- Drill bits.

81
00:07:03,211 --> 00:07:06,237
Take your drill bit money,
and why don't you put it in the bank?

82
00:07:06,341 --> 00:07:08,848
Because if you continue making the movie
the way that you are,

83
00:07:08,952 --> 00:07:11,371
there isn't gonna be a distributor
who'll want to distribute it.

84
00:07:11,476 --> 00:07:14,078
You're not gonna find anybody
who wants to see the movie,

85
00:07:14,182 --> 00:07:17,168
and you're not gonna have
any more oil money.

86
00:07:17,272 --> 00:07:20,376
So, welcome to Hollywood.

87
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:23,965
Yeah. Well, I'll be sure
to remember that, Mr Mayer.

88
00:07:24,069 --> 00:07:26,092
- Good luck.
- All right.

89
00:07:26,197 --> 00:07:29,355
He needs 26 to make it work?
He's out of his mind.

90
00:07:36,449 --> 00:07:40,363
I looked down at my pants.
It was a strange situation.

91
00:07:41,162 --> 00:07:42,162
Boss.

92
00:07:42,375 --> 00:07:46,489
Scram, love boat, let's go.
Another soda. You know what I mean.

93
00:07:46,593 --> 00:07:49,492
Milk, please, in the bottle.
With the cap still on.

94
00:07:49,596 --> 00:07:51,224
OK, Howard, what did "His Highness" say?

95
00:07:51,328 --> 00:07:54,592
The son of a bitch won't part with a single
goddamn camera. Can you believe it?

96
00:07:54,717 --> 00:07:57,996
Howard, why don't you try
and make do with what you have?

97
00:07:58,100 --> 00:08:02,418
What I have isn't enough, John.
Not for how I see it. Look.

98
00:08:02,522 --> 00:08:04,131
My name depends on this picture.

99
00:08:04,236 --> 00:08:06,906
If it doesn't work, I'm back to Houston
with my tail between my legs,

100
00:08:07,010 --> 00:08:09,510
making goddamn drill bits
for the rest of my life.

101
00:08:09,614 --> 00:08:11,911
Couldn't you find a way to do it
with the cameras you have?

102
00:08:12,015 --> 00:08:14,997
I mean, jeez, you ought to hear what's
going on with DeMille over at Paramount.

103
00:08:15,102 --> 00:08:17,771
He's shooting this Bible picture, right,
and he's gotta do a crucifixion.

104
00:08:17,875 --> 00:08:19,355
- Johnny.
- He's doing it in Fresno.

105
00:08:19,459 --> 00:08:21,316
- Johnny. Johnny.
- All of a sudden there's...

106
00:08:21,421 --> 00:08:22,900
- Johnny.
- Yeah?

107
00:08:23,004 --> 00:08:25,359
- You're a press agent, are you not?
- Yeah.

108
00:08:25,463 --> 00:08:28,863
And you're supposed to know all the little
ins and outs of Hollywood? Do you?

109
00:08:28,968 --> 00:08:34,243
- Yeah, absolutely.
- Then you leave the big ideas to me.

110
00:08:34,347 --> 00:08:37,257
- Yeah, of course, boss.
- Cigar, cigarettes, Sen-Sen?

111
00:08:38,302 --> 00:08:42,625
Thelma. I thought you were over at
the Brown Derby with Trixie. No, Theresa.

112
00:08:42,729 --> 00:08:45,503
- Margaret, yeah.
- Exactly. What happened to Margaret?

113
00:08:45,607 --> 00:08:48,132
- She lost her shoes?
- Thank God that's settled.

114
00:08:48,236 --> 00:08:51,636
I'm sorry.
Thelma, this is Howard Hughes.

115
00:08:51,740 --> 00:08:56,349
Howard and I were just discussing how he
wants me to pull a camera out of my ass.

116
00:08:56,454 --> 00:09:01,107
- Cigarette?
- Oh, no, thanks. I don't smoke.

117
00:09:02,284 --> 00:09:06,441
Whew. Boy, you are just hitting
on all six cylinders, aren't you?

118
00:09:06,545 --> 00:09:07,945
My God.

119
00:09:09,129 --> 00:09:11,361
Would you do me a favour and just...

120
00:09:11,465 --> 00:09:14,995
Would you just smile for me one time?
Just once?

121
00:09:17,049 --> 00:09:20,290
Whew. Yeah. Yeah.
You see, you got a short upper lip.

122
00:09:20,394 --> 00:09:23,671
Makes for a much nicer smile.

123
00:09:27,268 --> 00:09:31,008
See, I wonder what gives
a beautiful woman like you pleasure.

124
00:09:31,112 --> 00:09:34,845
I mean, say you're just standing there,
right? And I just touch you.

125
00:09:34,949 --> 00:09:38,932
Just like this,
through my fingertips.

126
00:09:39,037 --> 00:09:42,354
Do you...
Do you like that?

127
00:09:42,458 --> 00:09:43,858
Do you?

128
00:09:49,081 --> 00:09:53,072
See, I want to learn what pleases you.

129
00:09:53,176 --> 00:09:56,531
I want to learn everything about you.

130
00:09:56,636 --> 00:10:01,173
Would you let me do that?
Would you give me that job?

131
00:10:06,056 --> 00:10:08,049
I'm off in a half an hour.

132
00:10:09,643 --> 00:10:12,423
- Well, I'm in room...
- 217.

133
00:10:12,528 --> 00:10:13,328
217.

134
00:10:16,484 --> 00:10:19,188
See you there.

135
00:10:22,782 --> 00:10:25,145
Johnny, get on the horn
to Universal and Warner's.

136
00:10:25,250 --> 00:10:28,234
I need two more cameras by Saturday.
Rent 'em if you can.

137
00:10:28,338 --> 00:10:30,988
Steal 'em if you have to.

138
00:10:31,092 --> 00:10:34,747
Abso-tively, boss.
Abso-tively.

139
00:10:36,420 --> 00:10:39,412
Rumours coming from
a lonely airstrip out in Van Nuys.

140
00:10:39,516 --> 00:10:42,914
Sources whisper that young
Texas industrialist Howard Hughes

141
00:10:43,018 --> 00:10:46,750
just won't stop pouring money
into his war epic. And do we mean epic.

142
00:10:46,855 --> 00:10:52,879
137 pilots, 87 airplanes,
35 cameramen, 2,000 extras.

143
00:10:52,984 --> 00:10:56,315
How long will it be before this picture
ends up costing as much as the real war?

144
00:10:56,440 --> 00:10:59,515
Goddamn it.
Why the hell do they look so slow?

145
00:10:59,619 --> 00:11:05,862
This isn't what it was like up there. They
look like a bunch of goddamn models.

146
00:11:11,080 --> 00:11:12,788
Son of a bitch.

147
00:11:14,125 --> 00:11:15,525
Howard?

148
00:11:18,129 --> 00:11:22,074
Without something standing still
behind the planes

149
00:11:22,178 --> 00:11:24,660
we've got no idea of how fast we're moving.

150
00:11:24,764 --> 00:11:28,706
We got no sense of relative motion.

151
00:11:28,810 --> 00:11:31,420
Call over to UCLA.
Get me the best meteorologist they got.

152
00:11:31,524 --> 00:11:34,968
You get him here in an hour, all right?

153
00:11:37,273 --> 00:11:40,014
Hey. You want the good news
or the bad news?

154
00:11:40,118 --> 00:11:41,763
- The bad news, always.
- All right.

155
00:11:41,867 --> 00:11:44,891
We installed the 450 radial
but the struts won't take the vibration.

156
00:11:44,995 --> 00:11:49,228
Minute we fire her up, the struts
start cracking at the attach points.

157
00:11:49,333 --> 00:11:52,273
- Well, what's the good news?
- There isn't any.

158
00:11:52,377 --> 00:11:56,150
Goddamn it, Odie, if the 450's too big,
we just got to figure something else out.

159
00:11:56,254 --> 00:11:59,158
We've done everything.
We've rebuilt her from top to toe.

160
00:11:59,262 --> 00:12:03,032
Now, if we drain the fuel tank, for a couple
of runs, she might make 180mph.

161
00:12:03,136 --> 00:12:04,996
I want minimum 200, all right?

162
00:12:05,100 --> 00:12:07,730
I want a date with Theda Bara,
but that ain't gonna happen either.

163
00:12:07,834 --> 00:12:08,791
Don't be so sure.

164
00:12:08,895 --> 00:12:14,298
So if the struts won't sustain the engine
we need, we got to get rid of the struts.

165
00:12:14,403 --> 00:12:17,311
No, then the top wing falls off.

166
00:12:19,190 --> 00:12:21,176
Then let it.

167
00:12:21,280 --> 00:12:22,280
What?

168
00:12:23,699 --> 00:12:28,356
Who says we need a top wing?
I mean, who says we need anything?

169
00:12:30,534 --> 00:12:33,314
- A monoplane.
- A cantilevered monoplane, right.

170
00:12:33,418 --> 00:12:36,446
I mean, they're doing it in France.
To hell with the top wing and the struts.

171
00:12:36,551 --> 00:12:39,487
- Put in a 550 Wasp engine.
- 100 octane fuel.

172
00:12:39,591 --> 00:12:43,741
- That'd give us a top horsepower of what?
- 700.

173
00:12:43,845 --> 00:12:48,964
We squeeze that to a thousand,
we've got the fastest plane ever built.

174
00:12:50,846 --> 00:12:57,134
You know, I just got to say we've already
spent over $ 200,000 rebuilding this plane.

175
00:12:57,728 --> 00:13:00,387
To hell with it.

176
00:13:00,491 --> 00:13:03,517
Tear it up, Odie.
Go on.

177
00:13:06,862 --> 00:13:07,862
Well.

178
00:13:09,452 --> 00:13:15,035
The cumulonimbus formations
about which you speak...

179
00:13:16,372 --> 00:13:18,525
that look like...

180
00:13:18,629 --> 00:13:22,403
Giant breasts full of milk.
I want clouds, damn it.

181
00:13:22,507 --> 00:13:28,413
Yes, clouds that look like
giant breasts full of milk

182
00:13:28,517 --> 00:13:32,249
cannot exactly be guaranteed
for any particular occasion.

183
00:13:32,353 --> 00:13:34,718
So you might have to...

184
00:13:35,599 --> 00:13:37,199
to wait.

185
00:13:39,061 --> 00:13:41,924
Then we'll wait.

186
00:13:42,029 --> 00:13:46,474
Look, whatever they pay you at UCLA, I'm
doubling it, all right? You work for me now.

187
00:13:46,578 --> 00:13:49,766
Find some clouds.

188
00:13:49,870 --> 00:13:53,438
Find some clouds.
Find me some clouds.

189
00:13:53,542 --> 00:13:56,025
Welcome to <i>Hell's Angels.</i>

190
00:13:59,123 --> 00:14:00,238
Thank you.

191
00:14:10,009 --> 00:14:13,416
We've been to Chatsworth, Santa Cruz,
Encino, San Diego, Riverside,

192
00:14:13,521 --> 00:14:16,337
Van Nuys and Bakersfield.
It has been eight months.

193
00:14:16,441 --> 00:14:20,883
- Where are my goddamn clouds?
- They move, Mr Hughes.

194
00:14:20,987 --> 00:14:24,469
Clouds move.
That's what they do. They move.

195
00:14:24,573 --> 00:14:30,514
See that? It is costing me $5,271 a day
to keep those planes on the ground.

196
00:14:30,618 --> 00:14:34,361
You find me some goddamn clouds.

197
00:14:40,831 --> 00:14:42,631
Nice day.

198
00:14:42,800 --> 00:14:44,861
Yeah, very funny.

199
00:14:44,966 --> 00:14:47,974
Listen, I got a call from Houston.
They're getting real nervous about all this.

200
00:14:48,078 --> 00:14:51,326
- Stop showing them the damn bills, Noah.
- That would be illegal, Howard.

201
00:14:51,430 --> 00:14:53,994
Shit, no.
Maybe it's a little bit naughty.

202
00:14:54,099 --> 00:14:59,294
Hughes Tools is incorporated in Texas.
They have to see the bills.

203
00:14:59,399 --> 00:15:02,799
Incorporate a new division out here,
all right? We'll call it Hughes Aircraft.

204
00:15:02,903 --> 00:15:05,464
Odie, do we need
these rivets on the cowling?

205
00:15:05,568 --> 00:15:07,679
Yeah, or the reverse thrust would rip it off.

206
00:15:07,783 --> 00:15:10,177
They're gonna give me drag.
Do something about that, OK?

207
00:15:10,282 --> 00:15:12,623
- Wind resistance on the rivets?
- Yeah, I want her slippery.

208
00:15:12,728 --> 00:15:15,727
There are serious tax consequences
to incorporating in California.

209
00:15:15,831 --> 00:15:18,899
- Just take care of it, would you?
- Mr Hughes. Mr Hughes.

210
00:15:19,004 --> 00:15:23,279
Oakland.
We have clouds in Oakland.

211
00:15:23,383 --> 00:15:25,654
- You mean it this time?
- Yes.

212
00:15:25,758 --> 00:15:30,452
Goddamn it, yes. I can promise you.
Clouds in Oakland.

213
00:15:30,556 --> 00:15:32,435
All right, all right,
don't get all jittery now.

214
00:15:32,539 --> 00:15:35,939
Oakland.
Oakland.

215
00:15:36,768 --> 00:15:41,056
We're going to Oakland.
Oakland.

216
00:16:16,427 --> 00:16:18,918
Down and to the left.

217
00:16:42,244 --> 00:16:45,494
That was perfect.

218
00:16:52,421 --> 00:16:55,080
Yes, young Howard Hughes
has pulled it off.

219
00:16:55,184 --> 00:16:58,748
After two years, <i>Hell's Angels</i>
has finally finished filming.

220
00:16:58,853 --> 00:17:01,791
There's going to be one heck
of a wrap party in Hollywood tonight.

221
00:17:01,895 --> 00:17:04,880
The price tag?
A staggering $ 2 million.

222
00:17:04,985 --> 00:17:07,378
So, if every human being
in America buys a ticket,

223
00:17:07,482 --> 00:17:09,387
heck, he might
even make a profit.

224
00:17:14,401 --> 00:17:18,347
First the clouds don't come, then the
planes break down, then the planes crash.

225
00:17:18,451 --> 00:17:20,101
Then the Zeppelins don't work.

226
00:17:20,205 --> 00:17:23,066
Seems we've had everything but
a damn plague of locusts on this thing.

227
00:17:23,171 --> 00:17:24,605
I mean, you have to admit.

228
00:17:24,710 --> 00:17:30,493
Now, honestly, did you ever think
you'd actually finish the damn thing?

229
00:17:30,918 --> 00:17:32,460
Come with me.

230
00:17:34,130 --> 00:17:37,743
Mama, darling,
if I'm a success in this show,

231
00:17:37,847 --> 00:17:39,844
- well, we're going to move from here.
- Oh, no.

232
00:17:39,969 --> 00:17:42,000
Oh, yes.
We're gonna move up in the Bronx.

233
00:17:42,104 --> 00:17:44,609
Lot of nice green grass up there
and a whole lot of people you know.

234
00:17:44,713 --> 00:17:47,087
There's the Ginsbergs,
the Guttenbergs, and the Goldbergs.

235
00:17:47,192 --> 00:17:49,019
A whole lot of bergs.
I don't know them all.

236
00:17:50,437 --> 00:17:54,383
You see, this is what the people want.
Silent pictures are yesterday's news.

237
00:17:54,487 --> 00:17:58,101
So I figure we gotta reshoot
<i>Hell's Angels</i> for sound.

238
00:17:59,738 --> 00:18:02,647
- How much of it?
- All of it.

239
00:18:02,751 --> 00:18:07,897
Before you even ask, I'll tell you. An
additional 1.7 million. We got that much?

240
00:18:08,001 --> 00:18:11,237
- No.
- Well, we'll make it.

241
00:18:11,342 --> 00:18:13,365
Take care of that, would you?

242
00:18:13,469 --> 00:18:14,659
Now get this.

243
00:18:24,972 --> 00:18:29,173
In the halls of 7000 Romaine, it goes on
day after day, month after month.

244
00:18:29,277 --> 00:18:33,135
Howard Hughes is now editing
some 25 miles of film.

245
00:18:34,899 --> 00:18:39,516
Heck, I say release it now and give
the world its first 560-hour movie.

246
00:18:39,620 --> 00:18:44,683
Pat. Mr Hughes needs this reel
in the projection room right now.

247
00:18:44,788 --> 00:18:46,482
Enough is enough, Mr Hughes.

248
00:18:46,586 --> 00:18:49,690
Are you ever gonna let us actually see
this little epic of yours?

249
00:18:49,795 --> 00:18:51,595
Hurry up.

250
00:19:12,311 --> 00:19:13,759
- Who is it?
- Noah.

251
00:19:13,864 --> 00:19:15,930
Come on in.

252
00:19:17,149 --> 00:19:19,485
I've been on the phone to Houston
for three solid hours.

253
00:19:19,610 --> 00:19:21,513
We're fixing every goddamn book we have.

254
00:19:21,617 --> 00:19:23,810
Wait, wait, wait.

255
00:19:23,914 --> 00:19:27,314
Yeah. Run reel ten again.
I think we're duplicating a shot here.

256
00:19:27,418 --> 00:19:31,233
And tell Jimmy I want
ten chocolate chip cookies, all right?

257
00:19:31,337 --> 00:19:35,031
Medium chips,
none too close to the outside. Got it?

258
00:19:36,043 --> 00:19:38,529
Do you remember
that goddamn shot from reel ten?

259
00:19:38,633 --> 00:19:43,662
No. I don't remember anything from
reel ten. I don't know what reel ten is.

260
00:19:43,766 --> 00:19:47,000
I'm a businessman, Howard,
and so are you.

261
00:19:47,104 --> 00:19:48,916
Now, look.

262
00:19:49,021 --> 00:19:52,960
This has been a great ride.
We've had a hell of a lot of fun.

263
00:19:53,064 --> 00:19:56,922
But you're losing
$ 25,000 a day doing this.

264
00:19:57,027 --> 00:19:59,027
Every day.

265
00:20:04,655 --> 00:20:07,442
So, what are my options?

266
00:20:09,034 --> 00:20:11,693
Well, this time I don't know
that you have any.

267
00:20:11,798 --> 00:20:14,862
I'm afraid you got to close it down.

268
00:20:14,966 --> 00:20:16,700
Dig your way out.

269
00:20:23,257 --> 00:20:25,788
I'm sorry, Howard.

270
00:20:25,892 --> 00:20:28,092
I truly am.

271
00:20:35,060 --> 00:20:38,429
Reel ten, Mr Hughes.

272
00:20:42,693 --> 00:20:45,556
Mortgage Toolco.

273
00:20:45,661 --> 00:20:47,974
Every asset.

274
00:20:48,079 --> 00:20:50,771
- You heard me.
- If you do that, you could lose everything.

275
00:20:50,875 --> 00:20:52,944
Well, I won't.

276
00:20:55,581 --> 00:20:57,181
I won't.

277
00:20:58,344 --> 00:21:00,344
All right.

278
00:21:01,545 --> 00:21:03,289
I'll get into it.

279
00:21:04,548 --> 00:21:06,707
Thanks, Noah.

280
00:21:41,252 --> 00:21:44,947
More than half a million good souls
lining the curb of Hollywood Boulevard.

281
00:21:45,051 --> 00:21:46,914
Look at the automobiles,
ladies and gentlemen.

282
00:21:47,018 --> 00:21:50,290
There are at least 45,000 cars here,
making it the greatest traffic jam...

283
00:21:50,394 --> 00:21:53,461
Young Howard Hughes must have
quite a lump in his throat tonight.

284
00:21:53,565 --> 00:21:57,590
Six months after the stock market crashes
and after the death of three of his pilots,

285
00:21:57,694 --> 00:22:00,798
he's finally unveiling
his $4-million baby doll.

286
00:22:00,903 --> 00:22:05,264
It's the most expensive movie ever.
Nothing five-and-dime for our Mr Hughes.

287
00:22:05,368 --> 00:22:07,474
Nothing like tonight
has ever been seen before,

288
00:22:07,578 --> 00:22:10,600
and I can certainly say
it will never be seen again.

289
00:22:10,704 --> 00:22:13,644
500,000 people are crowding the streets
to get a glimpse

290
00:22:13,749 --> 00:22:17,317
of the stars and celebrities.
Three companies of Marines were called

291
00:22:17,421 --> 00:22:21,236
to assist the 250 special police
who are handling this enormous crowd.

292
00:22:21,341 --> 00:22:22,904
This is an industry town, my friends,

293
00:22:23,008 --> 00:22:26,326
and nobody, but nobody,
makes a movie outside a studio.

294
00:22:26,430 --> 00:22:28,908
Sure, some Hollywood insiders
over at the Brown Derby...

295
00:22:29,012 --> 00:22:31,161
And now, ladies and gentlemen, I think...

296
00:22:31,265 --> 00:22:34,374
Yes, yes. I can just see
Mr Hughes's car arriving now.

297
00:22:34,478 --> 00:22:38,212
Mr Hughes escorts
the lovely starlet Jean Harlow tonight.

298
00:22:38,316 --> 00:22:41,965
He discovered her for this picture,
and we think her platinum-blonde locks

299
00:22:42,070 --> 00:22:46,724
and hot-jazz, baby-doll style
are gonna make her a big star.

300
00:23:11,842 --> 00:23:13,035
Mr Hughes.

301
00:23:13,139 --> 00:23:16,498
Mr Hughes, how about a word?

302
00:23:16,602 --> 00:23:21,048
Big night for you, Mr Hughes.

303
00:23:21,153 --> 00:23:24,051
Big night for you tonight.

304
00:23:24,156 --> 00:23:26,261
Very big.
Very big.

305
00:23:26,365 --> 00:23:30,485
Tell us what it was like
making this fabulous picture.

306
00:23:31,987 --> 00:23:32,787
Yes.

307
00:23:33,874 --> 00:23:34,874
Yeah.

308
00:23:35,417 --> 00:23:41,197
So, four million clams from your own
pocket. Nervous how the flick will fly?

309
00:23:43,415 --> 00:23:47,239
Big night.
You enjoy the show.

310
00:23:47,343 --> 00:23:52,830
Yes, well, let me present the feminine star
of this spectacle, <i>Hell's Angels.</i>

311
00:23:52,934 --> 00:23:55,411
- Miss Jean Harlow.
- Thank you.

312
00:23:55,515 --> 00:23:58,708
I would like to use this occasion
to publicly thank Mr Hughes

313
00:23:58,812 --> 00:24:02,335
for the opportunity he gave me.
Thank you.

314
00:24:02,440 --> 00:24:06,297
Thank you. And now
I'd like to ask Roscoe Arbuckle

315
00:24:06,401 --> 00:24:08,761
to introduce his pet lion cub, Gilmore.

316
00:24:08,865 --> 00:24:11,329
What the hell's the matter with you?
Can't you remember my name?

317
00:24:11,433 --> 00:24:15,731
I'm sorry. Roscoe Turner.
And this would be Gilmore.

318
00:24:58,783 --> 00:25:00,526
We've got him.
We've got him.

319
00:25:02,119 --> 00:25:05,316
Murder. That's what
this dirty rotten politician war is.

320
00:25:05,420 --> 00:25:07,288
Murder. You know it as well as I do.

321
00:25:45,037 --> 00:25:47,706
Stand up, Slim, take a bow.

322
00:26:00,177 --> 00:26:02,292
Reel four played way too long.
Too many coughs.

323
00:26:02,396 --> 00:26:04,984
Get Harry and the team out of the party,
get them over to the office.

324
00:26:05,088 --> 00:26:06,377
I want to cut a few shots tonight.

325
00:26:06,481 --> 00:26:10,299
Find Glenn Odekirk. Somebody write
this down. "Flush rivets." You got that?

326
00:26:10,403 --> 00:26:12,260
- Flush rivets.
- Flush rivets.

327
00:26:12,364 --> 00:26:14,055
Lickety split, boss.

328
00:26:14,159 --> 00:26:16,226
Here he is.

329
00:26:17,862 --> 00:26:20,264
<i>Variety</i> says,
"This one won't miss."

330
00:26:20,368 --> 00:26:22,644
Magnificently photographed...

331
00:26:22,749 --> 00:26:26,188
Scenes that never
have been approached...

332
00:26:26,292 --> 00:26:29,611
Not for a long time have I seen
anything as enthralling...

333
00:26:29,715 --> 00:26:34,665
It cost four million dollars
and it has four million thrills...

334
00:27:31,185 --> 00:27:37,057
The lighter shade. I don't want to look like
Boris Karloff, if you know what I mean.

335
00:27:38,108 --> 00:27:42,016
I read in the magazines
that you play golf.

336
00:27:42,120 --> 00:27:43,811
On occasion.

337
00:27:43,915 --> 00:27:46,397
Well, how about nine holes?

338
00:27:48,160 --> 00:27:50,940
Now, Mr Hughes?

339
00:27:51,044 --> 00:27:54,074
If it would be convenient, Miss Hepburn.

340
00:28:16,230 --> 00:28:19,760
You're not extending enough
on your follow-through.

341
00:28:19,864 --> 00:28:23,099
Follow-through is everything in golf,
just like life. Ha-ha-ha.

342
00:28:23,204 --> 00:28:24,350
Don't you find?

343
00:28:24,454 --> 00:28:26,516
Saw your <i>Scarface</i> picture.

344
00:28:26,621 --> 00:28:28,436
- Violent.
- Realistic.

345
00:28:28,540 --> 00:28:33,772
Movies are movies, Howard, not life.
Now, the stage - the stage is real.

346
00:28:33,877 --> 00:28:38,074
Real flesh and blood. Human beings
right up there in front of you, buster.

347
00:28:38,178 --> 00:28:41,282
Can't look away.
Can't munch popcorn. That would be rude.

348
00:28:41,386 --> 00:28:43,244
- Do you like the theatre?
- No.

349
00:28:43,349 --> 00:28:45,538
I adore the theatre.

350
00:28:45,643 --> 00:28:47,832
Only alive on stage.
I'll teach you.

351
00:28:47,937 --> 00:28:51,963
We'll see some Ibsen, if the Republicans
haven't outlawed him by now. Ha-ha.

352
00:28:52,067 --> 00:28:55,196
You're not a Republican, are you? Couldn't
abide that. How did you vote in '32?

353
00:28:55,300 --> 00:28:59,391
- Well, I didn't.
- You must. It's your sacred franchise.

354
00:29:02,234 --> 00:29:05,392
Heard you were wooing Ginger Rogers.
What about that?

355
00:29:05,496 --> 00:29:07,225
Aw, she's just a friend.

356
00:29:07,329 --> 00:29:09,937
Ha. Men can't be friends
with women, Howard.

357
00:29:10,041 --> 00:29:13,979
They must possess them or leave them be.
It's a primitive urge from caveman days.

358
00:29:14,084 --> 00:29:17,359
It's all in Darwin.
Hunt the flesh, kill the flesh, eat the flesh.

359
00:29:17,463 --> 00:29:21,320
That's the male sex all over.

360
00:29:21,424 --> 00:29:23,424
Excuse me?

361
00:29:23,594 --> 00:29:28,744
Well, if you're deaf you must own up to it.
Get a hearing aid, or see my father.

362
00:29:28,848 --> 00:29:32,540
He's a urologist, but it's all tied up
inside the body, don't you find?

363
00:29:32,644 --> 00:29:36,796
Me, I keep healthy.
I take seven showers a day to keep clean.

364
00:29:36,900 --> 00:29:40,589
Also because I am what's so
vulgarly referred to as "outdoorsy".

365
00:29:40,694 --> 00:29:44,552
Well, I'm not "outdoorsy".
I'm athletic. I sweat.

366
00:29:44,656 --> 00:29:47,432
There it is.
Now we both know the sordid truth.

367
00:29:47,536 --> 00:29:50,060
I sweat and you're deaf.

368
00:29:50,165 --> 00:29:53,526
Aren't we a fine pair of misfits?

369
00:29:56,622 --> 00:29:57,822
Three.

370
00:30:05,965 --> 00:30:08,041
Noble effort.

371
00:30:10,094 --> 00:30:13,373
So I suppose you're wooing me now.

372
00:30:13,477 --> 00:30:15,277
Oh, well.

373
00:30:34,493 --> 00:30:39,693
Not enough. Not enough.
These rivets have to be completely flush.

374
00:30:39,797 --> 00:30:42,948
I want every screw and joint countersunk.

375
00:30:43,052 --> 00:30:45,741
I want no wind resistance on the fuselage.

376
00:30:45,845 --> 00:30:49,162
She has got to be clean, Odie.
Clean. Understand?

377
00:30:49,266 --> 00:30:52,998
- OK, Howard.
- I don't know what else to tell you.

378
00:30:53,102 --> 00:30:54,333
What have you got for me?

379
00:30:54,437 --> 00:30:58,004
The thing is, TWA needs a new plane.
A modern plane.

380
00:30:58,108 --> 00:31:00,337
- Yeah? What kind of plane?
- OK.

381
00:31:00,441 --> 00:31:03,760
The DC-3 has 21 daytime seats
and 14 overnight berths.

382
00:31:03,864 --> 00:31:06,258
- Something bigger.
- Try 50 seats.

383
00:31:06,363 --> 00:31:09,389
- With a ceiling of 12,000 feet.
- No.

384
00:31:09,493 --> 00:31:13,227
20,000. Think about it, Jack.
What does 20,000 feet give you?

385
00:31:13,331 --> 00:31:15,774
- Less turbulence.
- Right, because it's above the weather.

386
00:31:15,878 --> 00:31:18,482
Jack, we want to fly above the weather.

387
00:31:18,586 --> 00:31:22,155
Only 1 per cent of the American population
has ever set foot on a commercial airliner.

388
00:31:22,260 --> 00:31:25,241
Why? Because they're scared to death,
Jack, and they should be.

389
00:31:25,345 --> 00:31:28,033
I mean, 7,000 feet is bumpy as shit.
You know that.

390
00:31:28,137 --> 00:31:30,661
We build a plane
that flies above the weather,

391
00:31:30,765 --> 00:31:34,620
we could get every man, woman and child
in this country to feel safe up there.

392
00:31:34,724 --> 00:31:37,877
An airplane with the ability
to fly in the substratosphere.

393
00:31:37,982 --> 00:31:41,253
Across the country.
Across the world.

394
00:31:41,357 --> 00:31:43,754
Now, that is the future.

395
00:31:43,858 --> 00:31:45,092
- You with me?
- Yeah.

396
00:31:45,196 --> 00:31:47,410
Shoot straight with me now,
cos I don't wanna get into all this

397
00:31:47,514 --> 00:31:49,261
if your board doesn't have the balls for it.

398
00:31:49,365 --> 00:31:52,019
- Would they support us?
- I don't know. They're tight bastards.

399
00:31:52,123 --> 00:31:54,146
- Well, what's your financial picture?
- Not great.

400
00:31:54,250 --> 00:31:56,354
- Last year's deficit?
- 770,000.

401
00:31:56,459 --> 00:31:59,064
- What's it selling at?
- About eight dollars a share.

402
00:31:59,169 --> 00:32:02,985
That's the lowest it's been.

403
00:32:03,089 --> 00:32:05,529
- I could do that.
- Do what?

404
00:32:05,633 --> 00:32:07,863
- Buy it.
- You want to buy the airline?

405
00:32:07,967 --> 00:32:10,787
For crying out loud, Jack. We don't want
a bunch of damn pencil-pushers

406
00:32:10,892 --> 00:32:13,287
getting in the way
of us making our plane, do we?

407
00:32:13,391 --> 00:32:18,206
Give me brass tacks, now. What does
controlling interest in TWA cost me?

408
00:32:18,311 --> 00:32:21,962
- Call it 15 million.
- Whew.

409
00:32:22,066 --> 00:32:25,013
- That is a chunk of change.
- Yeah.

410
00:32:27,773 --> 00:32:31,846
You call Noah Dietrich.
You have him start buying.

411
00:32:31,950 --> 00:32:36,267
Howard, hold on. Are you sure? You want
to think about it for five minutes?

412
00:32:36,371 --> 00:32:40,363
Hell, Jack, I've got a tiger by the tail here,
and I ain't gonna let it go.

413
00:32:49,170 --> 00:32:54,453
Good evening, Mr Hughes.
Welcome. Your table is ready.

414
00:32:54,557 --> 00:32:57,580
- How goes the aviation, Mr Hughes?
- Just fine, Pete.

415
00:32:57,684 --> 00:33:00,050
I'm so glad.

416
00:33:01,599 --> 00:33:04,340
- Good evening, Mr Hughes. Madame.
- It's "Miss".

417
00:33:04,444 --> 00:33:07,881
- Miss. The usual, Mr Hughes?
- Please, yes.

418
00:33:07,985 --> 00:33:10,427
And may I recommend for the lady
our clementine soup,

419
00:33:10,532 --> 00:33:14,431
followed by roast wild duck with currant
glaze and poached pears in rose sauce?

420
00:33:14,536 --> 00:33:17,731
- It's truly divine.
- Yeah. That sounds fine.

421
00:33:19,700 --> 00:33:23,562
Your kind of a joint, is it?

422
00:33:23,667 --> 00:33:26,110
- Wouldn't have thought.
- They're open late.

423
00:33:26,214 --> 00:33:30,069
I go to a hot dog stand on La Cienega, too.
They're open till about four.

424
00:33:30,173 --> 00:33:31,973
Are they?

425
00:33:33,464 --> 00:33:35,919
How marvellous.

426
00:33:51,148 --> 00:33:54,140
Howard. Howard.

427
00:33:54,244 --> 00:33:56,598
Hi. Son of a gun.

428
00:33:56,703 --> 00:34:00,602
Kate, this is Johnny Meyer. I suppose
you could call him my press agent.

429
00:34:00,707 --> 00:34:02,420
Pleased to meet you.
Loved you in <i>Alice Adams.</i>

430
00:34:02,524 --> 00:34:04,314
- You're too kind.
- I'm sure you know Errol, right?

431
00:34:04,419 --> 00:34:07,153
- Mr Flynn. Yes.
- Kate. Kate.

432
00:34:07,257 --> 00:34:11,320
Kate of the clenched-jawed Hepburns.
Enchanting as always.

433
00:34:11,425 --> 00:34:13,782
You should use Lux on your hands,
by the way. I do.

434
00:34:13,887 --> 00:34:17,080
Kate, sweetie, you and Howard ought
to cook up a picture. Co-star with Errol...

435
00:34:17,185 --> 00:34:18,751
My chair.

436
00:34:22,304 --> 00:34:25,667
I think not.
Don't you read <i>Variety.</i> Mr Meyer?

437
00:34:25,771 --> 00:34:29,423
I'm box-office poison.
I'm on the outs, the skids, the doldrums.

438
00:34:29,527 --> 00:34:33,260
Washed up, day-old fish
not worth the eating, so they tell me.

439
00:34:33,364 --> 00:34:36,887
To hell with them. To hell with them,
my dear. Soulless pricks to a man, right?

440
00:34:36,991 --> 00:34:43,273
Johnny tells me you're thinking about
doing a Western, of all goddamn things.

441
00:34:45,286 --> 00:34:48,066
Are you making a Western, Howard?

442
00:34:48,170 --> 00:34:50,778
Yeah. Making a Western.
I'm gonna call it <i>The Outlaw.</i>

443
00:34:50,882 --> 00:34:55,114
Yeah. And you know what it's about?
S-e-x. It's all about s-e-x.

444
00:34:55,219 --> 00:34:56,949
It's a Western.

445
00:34:57,054 --> 00:35:01,204
- You can't have fornication in a Western.
- It's not real sex, Skinny. It's movie sex.

446
00:35:01,308 --> 00:35:05,042
What <i>Scarface</i> did for the gangster picture,
<i>The Outlaw</i> is gonna do for the Western.

447
00:35:05,146 --> 00:35:08,086
Put the sex and guts and blood
right up there on the screen...

448
00:35:08,190 --> 00:35:10,298
- Have any cigarettes?
- Don't mind us.

449
00:35:10,402 --> 00:35:13,840
New York-cut steak, 12 peas,
bottle of milk with the cap on.

450
00:35:13,945 --> 00:35:17,219
You're the only movie star I know
can't afford his own goddamn cigarettes.

451
00:35:17,323 --> 00:35:21,686
I hope your food isn't getting cold over
at your table somewhere, or something.

452
00:35:21,790 --> 00:35:24,183
No, no, no. We're here all night.
Don't worry.

453
00:35:24,287 --> 00:35:27,857
Now, Howard.
Now, Howard, Howard. Howard.

454
00:35:27,962 --> 00:35:31,361
If you're seriously talking about putting
carnality back on the silver screen,

455
00:35:31,465 --> 00:35:36,035
you must swear to let me in
on the casting sessions.

456
00:35:37,922 --> 00:35:41,119
I've somewhat of an eye -
an eye for talent, isn't that right, Johnny?

457
00:35:41,223 --> 00:35:43,792
You ought to give up that prancing
in tights, be a talent scout.

458
00:35:43,896 --> 00:35:48,087
That prancing in tights paid for my new
yacht. You must all come sailing with me.

459
00:35:48,191 --> 00:35:49,756
Catalina, this weekend.
What do you say?

460
00:35:49,861 --> 00:35:52,087
- Yeah, Catalina. Sounds grand.
- Let's have some fun.

461
00:35:52,191 --> 00:35:54,845
I've even managed to coax
the luscious Miss de Havilland

462
00:35:54,949 --> 00:35:57,219
and her equally luscious sister
to accompany me.

463
00:35:57,323 --> 00:35:59,537
Though I fear their mother
will insist on coming along

464
00:35:59,641 --> 00:36:02,141
to preserve their questionable virtue.

465
00:36:02,245 --> 00:36:06,938
We shall assault these twin monuments
of pristine Britannic beauty nonetheless.

466
00:36:07,042 --> 00:36:09,187
- What do you say, Howard?
- Yeah?

467
00:36:09,292 --> 00:36:10,690
- Yeah.
- Yeah?

468
00:36:10,794 --> 00:36:11,794
Yeah.

469
00:36:16,127 --> 00:36:18,447
I gotta... I gotta go.

470
00:36:18,551 --> 00:36:21,743
If you'll excuse us,
we have to be somewhere.

471
00:36:21,847 --> 00:36:24,620
You are somewhere, Howard,
you madman.

472
00:36:24,725 --> 00:36:27,749
- Somewhere else. Excuse us.
- Charmed, gentlemen.

473
00:36:27,853 --> 00:36:32,131
Do help yourself to the poached pears.
I hear they're divine.

474
00:36:32,235 --> 00:36:35,756
Well. Howard Hughes,
ladies and gentlemen.

475
00:36:35,860 --> 00:36:37,176
Was that meant for me?

476
00:36:37,280 --> 00:36:40,719
My hero.
God, all that Hollywood talk bores me silly.

477
00:36:40,823 --> 00:36:43,852
As if there aren't more important things
in the world. Mussolini, for one.

478
00:36:43,956 --> 00:36:48,022
- Where are we going, by the way?
- Do you feel like a little adventure?

479
00:36:48,126 --> 00:36:50,148
Do your worst, Mr Hughes.

480
00:36:50,252 --> 00:36:51,520
...you limey bastard.

481
00:36:51,625 --> 00:36:54,484
I'm a Tasmanian bastard,
you ignorant prick.

482
00:36:54,588 --> 00:36:56,658
That a way, Errol.

483
00:37:30,534 --> 00:37:33,314
That's Mr Mayer's house right there.

484
00:37:33,418 --> 00:37:36,698
Do you know where Jack Warner lives?

485
00:37:44,632 --> 00:37:48,494
- What's that on the steering wheel?
- Cellophane.

486
00:37:48,598 --> 00:37:52,878
If you had any idea of the crap
that people carry around on their hands...

487
00:37:52,983 --> 00:37:55,293
What kind of crap?

488
00:37:55,398 --> 00:37:57,599
You don't want to know.

489
00:38:02,233 --> 00:38:05,103
Hold onto the wheel for a bit.

490
00:38:12,409 --> 00:38:14,651
That's too hard.
Relax your hand. Relax your hand.

491
00:38:21,127 --> 00:38:26,282
You see, you've got to feel the vibration
of the engine through your fingertips.

492
00:38:26,386 --> 00:38:29,745
- Feel that?
- Yes.

493
00:38:29,849 --> 00:38:30,849
Yeah.

494
00:38:31,979 --> 00:38:34,169
Well, that's good.

495
00:38:34,273 --> 00:38:35,473
Golly.

496
00:38:40,437 --> 00:38:42,340
Well, she's all yours.

497
00:38:42,445 --> 00:38:46,929
- Where are you going?
- I think there's some milk back here.

498
00:38:47,034 --> 00:38:50,942
- You just keep us steady now.
- All right.

499
00:39:08,966 --> 00:39:10,748
- Howard.
- Yeah?

500
00:39:10,852 --> 00:39:13,245
There's a rather alarming mountain
heading our way.

501
00:39:13,350 --> 00:39:16,216
Pull back on the wheel a smidge.
Go on.

502
00:39:20,811 --> 00:39:22,011
Golly.

503
00:39:29,361 --> 00:39:35,067
I don't think I've ever met someone
who actually uses the word "golly".

504
00:39:41,457 --> 00:39:43,737
You all right?
Do you want me to take over?

505
00:39:43,842 --> 00:39:47,702
Just when I'm getting the hang of it?

506
00:39:51,509 --> 00:39:55,458
- You... You want some milk?
- Please.

507
00:40:17,159 --> 00:40:21,066
Utterly smashing.
We'll do it again. I'm free Wednesday.

508
00:40:21,171 --> 00:40:23,030
It's a little early for golf, though,
don't you think?

509
00:40:23,134 --> 00:40:25,489
Oh, no. I live right there.

510
00:40:25,594 --> 00:40:28,419
- Feel like a drink?
- Lead on.

511
00:40:29,463 --> 00:40:32,993
Now, that makes
for a challenging par four.

512
00:40:33,097 --> 00:40:37,209
My decorator picked out
the wallpaper and such.

513
00:40:37,314 --> 00:40:42,421
He's queer as a bed bug, but...
I just hate this room.

514
00:40:42,525 --> 00:40:44,755
Gives me the willies. Ugh.

515
00:40:44,859 --> 00:40:49,017
Feel I'm about to be swallowed up
by the latest issue of <i>Town & Country.</i>

516
00:40:57,575 --> 00:41:00,605
What room do you like?

517
00:41:00,709 --> 00:41:02,509
My study.

518
00:41:04,457 --> 00:41:06,533
Take me there.

519
00:41:22,641 --> 00:41:26,382
You are the tallest woman I know.

520
00:41:26,486 --> 00:41:29,093
And all sharp elbows and knees.

521
00:41:29,197 --> 00:41:30,597
Beware.

522
00:41:53,172 --> 00:41:56,208
Will you fly me to work tomorrow?

523
00:41:58,803 --> 00:42:01,638
It is tomorrow.

524
00:42:53,566 --> 00:42:55,264
Keep your eye on the fuel gauge.

525
00:42:55,368 --> 00:42:58,972
She's got a minimum of fuel to keep
her weight down. Two runs, that's it.

526
00:42:59,076 --> 00:43:02,126
After that, you're flying on vapours.
And then you crash and then you die.

527
00:43:02,231 --> 00:43:03,898
- Right.
- Just give her easy flying.

528
00:43:04,002 --> 00:43:06,856
Don't worry about speed.
And don't think about the record today.

529
00:43:06,960 --> 00:43:10,301
I wish to God you'd let someone else take
her up. You've got damn 20 test pilots...

530
00:43:10,405 --> 00:43:13,366
Hell, why should I let
someone else have all the fun?

531
00:43:13,470 --> 00:43:16,248
See you in a bit.

532
00:43:36,275 --> 00:43:37,875
Contact.

533
00:45:05,614 --> 00:45:07,414
Whoo-hoo.

534
00:45:07,750 --> 00:45:08,550
339.

535
00:45:10,911 --> 00:45:12,074
Goddamn.

536
00:45:34,518 --> 00:45:35,318
347.

537
00:45:36,654 --> 00:45:38,887
Whoo.
Son of a bitch.

538
00:46:11,347 --> 00:46:13,947
- 352.
- 352.

539
00:46:15,100 --> 00:46:16,345
Good girl.

540
00:46:25,945 --> 00:46:27,545
Damn it.

541
00:46:31,450 --> 00:46:34,647
Goddamn it.
Oh, God.

542
00:46:34,751 --> 00:46:38,244
- There goes our meal ticket.
- Come on.

543
00:47:18,122 --> 00:47:20,402
Howard. Howard.

544
00:47:20,507 --> 00:47:22,946
How did we do?

545
00:47:23,051 --> 00:47:26,989
352 on the last run.

546
00:47:27,093 --> 00:47:28,797
She'll go faster.

547
00:47:29,300 --> 00:47:31,580
Country mouse.

548
00:47:31,684 --> 00:47:33,164
Kate. Katie.

549
00:47:33,268 --> 00:47:35,338
Upstairs, city mouse.

550
00:47:38,017 --> 00:47:39,217
Hello.

551
00:47:39,486 --> 00:47:43,716
- Good Lord, what happened to you?
- Nothing. Hard landing. I cut my foot.

552
00:47:43,820 --> 00:47:46,677
Sit down. I'll take care of it.
You tell me everything.

553
00:47:46,782 --> 00:47:50,516
Well, you cannot imagine what it was like,
Katie. You cannot imagine the speed.

554
00:47:50,620 --> 00:47:52,682
She was like a winged bullet up there.

555
00:47:52,787 --> 00:47:56,854
- What did she make?
- Around...

556
00:47:56,959 --> 00:47:57,759
352.

557
00:48:05,377 --> 00:48:07,577
You did it.

558
00:48:08,141 --> 00:48:10,078
Fastest man on the planet.

559
00:48:10,182 --> 00:48:14,625
- Hot dog. I'm so proud of you.
- She did it, baby.

560
00:48:14,729 --> 00:48:18,255
- You knew she would.
- She was fine. She was just fine.

561
00:48:20,392 --> 00:48:22,848
- Now let me see your foot.
- Yeah.

562
00:48:23,813 --> 00:48:25,595
Good God.
You're covered in blood.

563
00:48:25,699 --> 00:48:28,886
No, no. That's just beet juice.
I crashed into a beet field.

564
00:48:28,991 --> 00:48:32,816
- What?
- Yeah, I crashed in a beet field.

565
00:48:35,324 --> 00:48:38,232
Let me get you cleaned up.

566
00:48:38,336 --> 00:48:40,442
Heavens, what is this?
Electrical tape?

567
00:48:40,546 --> 00:48:43,900
Yeah. Odie just sort of slammed it on there.

568
00:48:44,005 --> 00:48:50,038
But all I could think about was
getting home to see you.

569
00:48:53,676 --> 00:48:57,294
I am so proud of you.

570
00:49:01,100 --> 00:49:03,170
Now, this is going to sting a little bit.

571
00:49:03,274 --> 00:49:07,681
This is useless. Come to the bathroom.

572
00:49:08,732 --> 00:49:11,512
- Don't get beet juice on the carpet.
- I won't.

573
00:49:11,617 --> 00:49:14,445
- Too hot.
- Don't be a baby.

574
00:49:16,073 --> 00:49:19,648
Was the press there?

575
00:49:19,752 --> 00:49:22,152
Some, but...

576
00:49:23,080 --> 00:49:27,623
They're calling everyone.
Should be on the wires by now.

577
00:49:34,967 --> 00:49:37,167
What is it?

578
00:49:39,388 --> 00:49:40,388
Kate?

579
00:49:44,101 --> 00:49:47,631
I've been famous,
for better or worse,

580
00:49:47,735 --> 00:49:50,565
for a long time now, and...

581
00:49:52,026 --> 00:49:56,735
I wonder if you know
what it really means.

582
00:49:57,823 --> 00:50:01,647
Yeah. I had my fair share
of press on <i>Hell's Angels.</i>

583
00:50:01,751 --> 00:50:04,116
I'm used to it.

584
00:50:05,456 --> 00:50:07,056
Are you?

585
00:50:10,836 --> 00:50:12,912
Howard, we're...

586
00:50:15,549 --> 00:50:18,301
We're not like everyone else.

587
00:50:20,054 --> 00:50:25,631
Too many acute angles.
Too many eccentricities.

588
00:50:25,736 --> 00:50:30,965
We have to be very careful
not to let people in,

589
00:50:31,069 --> 00:50:34,019
or they'll make us into freaks.

590
00:50:34,985 --> 00:50:35,985
Kate.

591
00:50:37,037 --> 00:50:39,976
They can't get in here.
We're safe.

592
00:50:40,080 --> 00:50:43,110
They can always get in.

593
00:50:45,287 --> 00:50:48,739
When my brother killed himself,

594
00:50:49,792 --> 00:50:53,405
there were photographers at the funeral.

595
00:50:53,509 --> 00:50:56,421
There's no decency to it.

596
00:51:08,394 --> 00:51:10,303
You know, sometimes I...

597
00:51:13,983 --> 00:51:16,604
I get these feelings, Katie.

598
00:51:21,157 --> 00:51:24,027
I get these ideas, these...

599
00:51:26,579 --> 00:51:28,951
crazy ideas about...

600
00:51:31,500 --> 00:51:34,252
things that may not...

601
00:51:35,296 --> 00:51:38,658
things that may not really be there.

602
00:51:38,763 --> 00:51:39,763
Yeah.

603
00:51:46,974 --> 00:51:50,260
Sometimes I truly fear that I'm...

604
00:51:51,729 --> 00:51:54,729
losing my mind.

605
00:52:01,363 --> 00:52:03,854
And if I did, it would...

606
00:52:06,327 --> 00:52:09,244
It'd be like flying blind.

607
00:52:16,962 --> 00:52:19,962
You understand?

608
00:52:22,676 --> 00:52:25,796
You taught me to fly, Howard.

609
00:52:28,474 --> 00:52:30,965
I'll take the wheel.

610
00:52:46,158 --> 00:52:50,443
Smashing all records, Howard Hughes
outdoes Jules Verne's wildest dreams.

611
00:52:50,547 --> 00:52:53,695
Around the world from New York
to New York in four days,

612
00:52:53,799 --> 00:52:57,115
even beating Wiley Post's mark
by over three days.

613
00:52:57,219 --> 00:52:58,826
New aviation history is written

614
00:52:58,930 --> 00:53:02,661
when his Lockheed monoplane
returns swiftly and safely.

615
00:53:02,765 --> 00:53:06,831
A daring aviator.
A true pioneer of the world's airways.

616
00:53:06,935 --> 00:53:09,709
From New York to Paris,
he cuts Lindbergh's time in half.

617
00:53:09,813 --> 00:53:11,128
Then on to Moscow.

618
00:53:11,232 --> 00:53:15,759
35 hours out of New York, he roars
across Siberia's trackless wastes.

619
00:53:15,863 --> 00:53:20,472
60 hours out of New York, he heads
for Alaska, most hazardous hop of all.

620
00:53:20,576 --> 00:53:23,098
Continuing the terrific pace,
he comes home,

621
00:53:23,203 --> 00:53:25,681
bringing new laurels
to American aviation.

622
00:53:25,785 --> 00:53:30,194
Howard Hughes and his crew may find
more worlds to conq...

623
00:53:36,750 --> 00:53:39,138
You're not gonna believe this.
It just came across the wires.

624
00:53:39,243 --> 00:53:42,704
Howard Hughes
has bought control of TWA.

625
00:53:44,300 --> 00:53:47,208
I thought Mr Hughes
was flying around the world.

626
00:53:47,312 --> 00:53:52,297
Apparently, he did it while he was flying,
over the radio.

627
00:53:58,022 --> 00:54:02,806
I have heard some disquieting rumours
about Mr Hughes.

628
00:54:02,910 --> 00:54:07,611
I'd like to know everything
there is to know about Mr Hughes.

629
00:54:08,657 --> 00:54:12,869
I'd like you to attend
to that for me, thoroughly.

630
00:54:13,996 --> 00:54:16,610
Ah, the Pantages is glittering tonight.

631
00:54:16,714 --> 00:54:18,970
Right over here, Mr Hughes.
This way, over to the left.

632
00:54:19,074 --> 00:54:21,074
Mr Hughes. Mr Hughes,
how was your flight?

633
00:54:21,178 --> 00:54:23,558
Miss Hepburn, Miss Hepburn.
When are you gonna name the day?

634
00:54:23,662 --> 00:54:27,538
- Right over here, Mr Hughes.
- What's your next movie, Howard?

635
00:54:27,642 --> 00:54:31,248
Right here, Howard. How many more
records are you gonna set?

636
00:54:31,352 --> 00:54:35,292
Come on, Howard.
Give us a smile. It won't kill you.

637
00:54:35,397 --> 00:54:37,467
- Raise your head a little.
- Over here. Over here.

638
00:54:37,572 --> 00:54:39,218
Don't forget this side.

639
00:54:39,322 --> 00:54:41,471
Mr Hughes, when are you
gonna fly round the world again?

640
00:54:41,576 --> 00:54:45,433
You going to fly with Kate next time?
Did you get Ionely without her?

641
00:54:45,537 --> 00:54:47,978
Did you talk to Lindbergh
about your round-the-world flight yet?

642
00:54:48,082 --> 00:54:50,893
Mr Hughes,
where is Linda Darnell tonight?

643
00:54:50,998 --> 00:54:53,145
Right here.
Right here, Mr Hughes. That's it.

644
00:54:53,249 --> 00:54:56,484
Are you trying to be
more famous than Lindbergh?

645
00:54:56,589 --> 00:55:00,448
You know,
fame is supposed to be my turf.

646
00:55:02,461 --> 00:55:03,061
LB.

647
00:55:06,799 --> 00:55:09,496
If you don't get more distinguished
every time I see you.

648
00:55:09,601 --> 00:55:12,380
Ah, you look so beautiful.

649
00:55:20,229 --> 00:55:21,844
Is that true?

650
00:55:21,948 --> 00:55:26,316
...Mr Randolph Scott.
They make a handsome couple...

651
00:55:30,531 --> 00:55:32,395
Don't worry about it, Howard.

652
00:55:32,500 --> 00:55:34,606
She's just working the room.

653
00:55:34,710 --> 00:55:37,074
It's her job, baby.

654
00:55:38,372 --> 00:55:42,989
Sultry southern tigress
Ava Gardner dazzles the room tonight.

655
00:55:43,093 --> 00:55:47,961
She's the newest star in the MGM galaxy.
And believe you me...

656
00:55:51,510 --> 00:55:56,303
Jane Eyre has been selling
popcorn for over a hundred years, LB.

657
00:56:37,932 --> 00:56:39,132
Hello.

658
00:56:41,018 --> 00:56:42,218
Hello.

659
00:56:47,691 --> 00:56:50,527
Could you reach me a towel?

660
00:56:57,993 --> 00:56:58,793
I...

661
00:57:00,878 --> 00:57:04,157
I really can't do that.

662
00:57:05,751 --> 00:57:07,751
I'm sorry.

663
00:57:32,027 --> 00:57:36,600
I'm an idiot.
I'm a complete idiot and I'm sorry.

664
00:57:36,704 --> 00:57:37,808
- Forget it.
- No, no.

665
00:57:37,912 --> 00:57:42,231
I'm a vain, preening ass
without a single redeeming feature.

666
00:57:42,336 --> 00:57:45,942
That's not true.
You have very good teeth.

667
00:57:46,047 --> 00:57:47,647
Come on.

668
00:57:48,299 --> 00:57:50,530
I've got a better idea.
Take me flying.

669
00:57:50,635 --> 00:57:54,579
- Or better yet, I'll take you flying.
- Do your worst, Miss Hepburn.

670
00:58:03,100 --> 00:58:05,420
Don't be so squirmy, Howard.

671
00:58:05,524 --> 00:58:07,636
You're going to get on famously
with Father and Mother.

672
00:58:07,740 --> 00:58:11,477
And I'm sure they'll like you, too.
Once they get to know you.

673
00:58:19,575 --> 00:58:20,768
Hello. Hello.

674
00:58:20,872 --> 00:58:23,785
- Kath, hello.
- Kathy.

675
00:58:27,333 --> 00:58:29,262
Sweetheart, who's that
with the camera right there?

676
00:58:29,366 --> 00:58:33,202
That's my ex-husband, Ludlow. Father
and Mother are just mad about Luddie.

677
00:58:33,306 --> 00:58:36,077
What the hell is he doing here?

678
00:58:36,181 --> 00:58:38,539
He's here all the time.

679
00:58:38,643 --> 00:58:39,843
Hello.

680
00:58:41,847 --> 00:58:44,755
Sorry we're late. Mom.

681
00:58:44,860 --> 00:58:46,504
- Thank you.
- Dad.

682
00:58:46,608 --> 00:58:48,882
- Darling.
- Uncle Willy.

683
00:58:48,987 --> 00:58:52,470
Who've we got here?
Don't feel self-conscious.

684
00:58:52,575 --> 00:58:55,596
Hepburns. Hepburns.
Attention, please.

685
00:58:55,701 --> 00:58:58,058
This is Howard.

686
00:58:58,163 --> 00:59:01,269
Howard, welcome.

687
00:59:01,373 --> 00:59:03,905
Don't worry, he's had his lunch.

688
00:59:04,995 --> 00:59:07,193
He likes you.
That's unusual.

689
00:59:07,298 --> 00:59:10,447
We pay our devotion to the arts here.
A colony we created.

690
00:59:10,551 --> 00:59:13,157
Julian's a painter.
Abstract, of course.

691
00:59:13,261 --> 00:59:15,266
What the hell's the point
of painting something real

692
00:59:15,370 --> 00:59:18,161
when you can just take a picture
nowadays, don't you agree?

693
00:59:18,265 --> 00:59:21,167
Where do you stand on politics,
Mr Hughes?

694
00:59:21,271 --> 00:59:23,709
- Excuse me?
- We're all socialists here.

695
00:59:23,813 --> 00:59:26,013
We are not.

696
00:59:26,479 --> 00:59:29,180
You've met Mr Roosevelt.
What make you of him?

697
