You are a son of a bitch.

924
01:14:19,535 --> 01:14:22,240
Bob, you got something on your suit.

925
01:14:23,497 --> 01:14:24,497
Yeah.

926
01:14:25,255 --> 01:14:29,828
On your lapel there.
You got something on your lapel.

927
01:14:30,880 --> 01:14:32,956
Right there, Bob.

928
01:14:34,550 --> 01:14:38,207
You missed it.
Right there.

929
01:14:38,311 --> 01:14:40,418
Clean it off, would you?

930
01:14:40,523 --> 01:14:43,123
Here. Thanks.

931
01:14:46,270 --> 01:14:49,271
Now throw it away.

932
01:14:51,817 --> 01:14:54,355
No, over there.

933
01:14:59,617 --> 01:15:03,607
Thanks.
So, what do you call her?

934
01:15:03,711 --> 01:15:05,984
The Constellation.
But we can change that.

935
01:15:06,089 --> 01:15:07,863
No, no, no.

936
01:15:07,968 --> 01:15:10,628
It's pretty. I like her.

937
01:15:11,796 --> 01:15:15,786
So, what kind of deal can you give me?
What kind of deal can you give me?

938
01:15:15,890 --> 01:15:18,452
We'll give you the first 40 planes
off the assembly line.

939
01:15:18,556 --> 01:15:21,038
That should give us
about two years exclusivity with her?

940
01:15:21,142 --> 01:15:22,587
Hell, more than that.

941
01:15:22,691 --> 01:15:26,754
United and American don't have
the imagination for a plane like this.

942
01:15:26,858 --> 01:15:29,972
Two years ahead of Juan Trippe, then.

943
01:15:34,527 --> 01:15:38,275
- How much?
- 450,000 each.

944
01:15:38,906 --> 01:15:42,646
That's 18 million for the first 40.

945
01:15:42,750 --> 01:15:48,200
Hell, TWA can't afford that.
The damn airline's flat broke.

946
01:15:49,875 --> 01:15:52,580
Guess I'll just have
to pay for them myself.

947
01:15:53,295 --> 01:15:56,409
Build them, Bob.
Send the bill to Noah Dietrich.

948
01:15:56,513 --> 01:15:58,250
And thank you.

949
01:16:01,846 --> 01:16:03,832
Merry Christmas.

950
01:16:03,936 --> 01:16:07,560
You have just placed the largest order for
airplanes in the history of the damn planet.

951
01:16:08,435 --> 01:16:10,302
Lockheed just sent us a bill for $18 million.

952
01:16:10,406 --> 01:16:13,970
Now now, don't get all hysterical on me,
Noah. It isn't good for you.

953
01:16:14,074 --> 01:16:17,761
- This is a lot of money for some planes.
- Yes. Yes, I know it's a lot of money.

954
01:16:17,866 --> 01:16:21,518
It's too damn much. You think I've just got
$18 million in petty cash?

955
01:16:21,623 --> 01:16:23,690
I should have told you earlier.
It slipped my mind.

956
01:16:23,795 --> 01:16:26,401
- Slipped your mind? For God's sake.
- Right. Well, look...

957
01:16:26,506 --> 01:16:30,274
- How could $18 million slip your mind?
- I'll get back to you. Goodbye, now.

958
01:16:30,378 --> 01:16:33,624
Hey, honey.
What are you doing home?

959
01:16:37,882 --> 01:16:41,789
You're not one for tears
and, well, neither am I.

960
01:16:41,893 --> 01:16:45,337
So it's best to come out with it.

961
01:16:48,309 --> 01:16:53,054
I've met someone.
I've fallen in love and I'm moving out.

962
01:16:55,274 --> 01:16:59,930
If I could make it any more gentle, I would.

963
01:17:00,034 --> 01:17:02,434
But I can't.

964
01:17:02,747 --> 01:17:06,405
So there we both are.

965
01:17:10,706 --> 01:17:12,654
Let's be honest.

966
01:17:12,758 --> 01:17:17,240
It's all been a grand adventure,
but it couldn't possibly last.

967
01:17:17,344 --> 01:17:21,335
We're too alike, you and I.

968
01:17:23,344 --> 01:17:26,623
- You met someone?
- Someone more appropriate.

969
01:17:26,727 --> 01:17:28,928
To me, I mean.

970
01:17:31,602 --> 01:17:34,549
What does that mean,
more appropriate?

971
01:17:34,653 --> 01:17:39,219
Someone more attuned to my needs.

972
01:17:39,323 --> 01:17:42,936
Look at me, Katie.
Stop acting.

973
01:17:44,615 --> 01:17:46,691
I'm not acting.

974
01:17:51,330 --> 01:17:54,117
I wonder if you even know any more.

975
01:17:58,879 --> 01:18:00,955
Don't be unkind.

976
01:18:03,759 --> 01:18:04,959
You...

977
01:18:06,855 --> 01:18:09,855
You want to go?

978
01:18:11,809 --> 01:18:13,009
Go on.

979
01:18:14,360 --> 01:18:18,048
Actresses are cheap in this town, darling.
I got a lot of money.

980
01:18:18,153 --> 01:18:22,600
- Howard, please. This is beneath you.
- No, no, this is exactly me.

981
01:18:22,704 --> 01:18:25,604
You come in here out of the blue and
tell me you're leaving me just like that,

982
01:18:25,708 --> 01:18:27,933
and you have the nerve
to expect graciousness?

983
01:18:28,038 --> 01:18:33,483
I expect a little maturity. I expect you
to face the situation like an adult who...

984
01:18:33,587 --> 01:18:37,443
Don't talk down to me.
Don't you ever talk down to me.

985
01:18:37,547 --> 01:18:40,912
You are a movie star, nothing more.

986
01:19:52,952 --> 01:19:56,071
Don't answer it.

987
01:19:57,581 --> 01:19:59,446
What is it, Howard?

988
01:19:59,550 --> 01:20:01,550
Hey, Noah.

989
01:20:01,716 --> 01:20:04,951
I need you to get to Penney's
and buy me new clothes.

990
01:20:05,055 --> 01:20:07,078
- Penney's isn't open.
- Shit.

991
01:20:07,183 --> 01:20:11,497
- It's two in the morning.
- Yeah, that's right. Ah, well.

992
01:20:11,602 --> 01:20:13,086
First thing tomorrow, then, all right?

993
01:20:13,191 --> 01:20:16,210
I need two suits off the rack.
One light and one dark.

994
01:20:16,315 --> 01:20:19,755
Three white shirts and three pairs
of white tennis shoes. You got that?

995
01:20:19,859 --> 01:20:22,929
- Yeah.
- No, no. Make it Woolworths.

996
01:20:23,033 --> 01:20:26,553
- Woolworths.
- No, no. Penney's. Penney's.

997
01:20:26,657 --> 01:20:28,932
All right, then.

998
01:20:29,037 --> 01:20:32,520
I'll get into it as soon as I can, Howard.

999
01:20:32,625 --> 01:20:35,940
- Noah, do you have a recorder?
- No.

1000
01:20:36,045 --> 01:20:39,566
- Are you recording this conversation?
- No.

1001
01:20:39,670 --> 01:20:40,270
OK.

1002
01:20:41,468 --> 01:20:43,868
I trust you.

1003
01:20:44,010 --> 01:20:44,868
Howard...

1004
01:20:44,972 --> 01:20:47,742
Listen, I need those suits
first thing tomorrow, all right?

1005
01:20:47,846 --> 01:20:49,033
- All right.
- Wait, wait.

1006
01:20:49,137 --> 01:20:52,290
- Did I say Penney's or Woolworths?
- Penney's.

1007
01:20:52,394 --> 01:20:56,135
- Better make it Sears.
- All right, then. Sears.

1008
01:20:59,643 --> 01:21:01,719
I'm sorry, honey.

1009
01:21:02,980 --> 01:21:05,388
If I don't answer,
he'll just call back.

1010
01:21:26,962 --> 01:21:30,581
Stop there, if you please,
Miss Domergue.

1011
01:21:35,805 --> 01:21:39,628
Have you had surgery, Miss Domergue?

1012
01:21:39,732 --> 01:21:40,332
No.

1013
01:21:42,110 --> 01:21:45,353
Do you have scars of any kind?

1014
01:21:46,565 --> 01:21:47,165
No.

1015
01:21:49,777 --> 01:21:52,315
Wipe off your lipstick.

1016
01:21:56,492 --> 01:21:59,189
That's much better.

1017
01:21:59,294 --> 01:22:04,411
Now, you understand that you'd be
under contract to me, personally?

1018
01:22:05,209 --> 01:22:07,700
Do you know what that means?

1019
01:22:15,928 --> 01:22:18,633
Turn around for me.

1020
01:22:25,896 --> 01:22:27,896
Very nice.

1021
01:22:28,321 --> 01:22:30,641
You move well.

1022
01:22:32,945 --> 01:22:36,602
You live with your family, do you?

1023
01:22:36,706 --> 01:22:37,506
Yes.

1024
01:22:39,289 --> 01:22:41,689
That's nice.

1025
01:22:42,169 --> 01:22:45,109
Tell me something.

1026
01:22:45,214 --> 01:22:47,745
How old are you, Miss Domergue?

1027
01:22:50,463 --> 01:22:51,063
15.

1028
01:22:55,676 --> 01:22:58,001
Holy mother of God.

1029
01:23:16,322 --> 01:23:20,439
Well, a car picks me up
every morning at eight, and off I go.

1030
01:23:20,544 --> 01:23:26,361
I am getting my high-school diploma.
Howard thinks that education is important.

1031
01:23:26,465 --> 01:23:28,777
- That's right.
- And then after classes,

1032
01:23:28,881 --> 01:23:32,450
I'm off for elocution
and grooming and fittings.

1033
01:23:32,555 --> 01:23:34,622
Well, blow me down.

1034
01:23:35,716 --> 01:23:38,166
Don't tell me Pan Am's
working out of the Cocoanut Grove now?

1035
01:23:38,271 --> 01:23:40,208
- Hello, Jack.
- Hello, Juan.

1036
01:23:40,312 --> 01:23:41,958
Helen, good to see you.

1037
01:23:42,062 --> 01:23:44,670
- How are you, Howard?
- Good, thanks.

1038
01:23:44,774 --> 01:23:48,590
- This is Miss Faith Domergue.
- Sit down. Now, what you doing out here?

1039
01:23:48,695 --> 01:23:51,679
Well, I'm out this way to meet
with Douglas about the DC-4.

1040
01:23:51,783 --> 01:23:55,681
That's our new plane
and it is gonna be a pip, let me tell you.

1041
01:23:55,785 --> 01:23:59,224
- How's the Constellation coming?
- Good.

1042
01:23:59,328 --> 01:24:00,528
Great.

1043
01:24:01,875 --> 01:24:04,619
So, how about letting me steal a peek?

1044
01:24:06,455 --> 01:24:08,403
Don't think so.

1045
01:24:08,507 --> 01:24:12,361
You know, I should be cross with you.
You stole Ray Loewy from us.

1046
01:24:12,465 --> 01:24:14,328
He's doing our interior design.
That's right.

1047
01:24:14,432 --> 01:24:16,913
He was doing ours.

1048
01:24:18,467 --> 01:24:21,459
So, what are your colours?

1049
01:24:21,563 --> 01:24:23,345
Stop fishing.

1050
01:24:24,974 --> 01:24:29,130
So, you have buttons or zippers?

1051
01:24:29,234 --> 01:24:30,888
I'm sorry?

1052
01:24:32,690 --> 01:24:34,510
Buttons or zippers?

1053
01:24:34,615 --> 01:24:37,775
For the drapes on the sleeping berths.

1054
01:24:40,156 --> 01:24:41,756
Zippers.

1055
01:24:50,624 --> 01:24:52,224
Buttons?

1056
01:25:01,927 --> 01:25:05,002
So, I suppose you'll be
expanding on down to Mexico.

1057
01:25:05,107 --> 01:25:08,294
- Why do you say that?
- Well, your range is 3,000 miles.

1058
01:25:08,398 --> 01:25:11,339
I would imagine you'd expand
from Los Angeles to Mexico.

1059
01:25:11,443 --> 01:25:14,716
- And maybe on down to South America.
- Hey, that's a good idea.

1060
01:25:14,820 --> 01:25:18,267
- Anyone got a pen?
- Or across the Atlantic.

1061
01:25:20,446 --> 01:25:23,892
Now, honey, you've had enough of this
ice cream. You give me that spoon.

1062
01:25:23,996 --> 01:25:27,407
You don't want to get your gloves all sticky.

1063
01:25:28,454 --> 01:25:30,493
Isn't that too far?

1064
01:25:35,461 --> 01:25:39,126
New York to Newfoundland...

1065
01:25:40,382 --> 01:25:42,873
to Ireland...

1066
01:25:46,597 --> 01:25:48,397
to Paris.

1067
01:25:55,314 --> 01:25:56,314
Well,

1068
01:25:57,572 --> 01:26:01,767
Pan Am welcomes you.
We're overbooked as it is.

1069
01:26:01,872 --> 01:26:05,519
It's such a burden doing it all on your own,
let me tell you.

1070
01:26:05,623 --> 01:26:07,814
So, when's the Connie gonna be ready?

1071
01:26:07,918 --> 01:26:10,607
- Next year, maybe. DC-4?
- Next year.

1072
01:26:10,712 --> 01:26:13,984
Well, we look forward to her, then.

1073
01:26:14,088 --> 01:26:17,037
And I to the Connie.

1074
01:26:17,878 --> 01:26:21,785
I've ordered the next 40 after you.

1075
01:26:21,890 --> 01:26:23,203
It's Miss?

1076
01:26:23,308 --> 01:26:24,854
- Domergue.
- Domergue. Yes, of course.

1077
01:26:24,958 --> 01:26:27,502
Now, was that a rumba or a samba
you were doing out there, my dear?

1078
01:26:27,607 --> 01:26:30,543
- It was a samba.
- A samba, yes.

1079
01:26:30,647 --> 01:26:33,975
Howard, I never knew
you were such a good dancer.

1080
01:26:34,812 --> 01:26:37,054
Helen, Jack.

1081
01:26:38,482 --> 01:26:41,890
Good going, boss. You just gave away
our entire postwar strategy.

1082
01:26:41,994 --> 01:26:43,889
- He can't stop us.
- He's Pan Am, Howard.

1083
01:26:43,993 --> 01:26:46,276
He can stop anything.

1084
01:26:47,992 --> 01:26:49,775
Waiter, give me
the largest Scotch you've got.

1085
01:26:49,879 --> 01:26:54,489
I don't know what in the hell you're
so goddamn giddy about. Excuse me.

1086
01:28:45,609 --> 01:28:46,609
Jack.

1087
01:28:47,162 --> 01:28:49,770
All right. I want you to get in touch
with Mr Joyce and Mr Berg.

1088
01:28:49,874 --> 01:28:51,084
Those are my boys in Washington.

1089
01:28:51,188 --> 01:28:53,649
Set up a meeting with Jesse Jones,
he's the secretary of commerce.

1090
01:28:53,753 --> 01:28:55,149
- Old golfing buddy.
- Slow down.

1091
01:28:55,253 --> 01:28:57,646
We're gonna need terminals
in both Ireland and France,

1092
01:28:57,751 --> 01:28:59,175
and I want to get tax breaks from them.

1093
01:28:59,279 --> 01:29:02,238
If that shit-ass thinks that he owns
the entire goddamn world,

1094
01:29:02,343 --> 01:29:04,618
- he's got another think coming.
- Pan Am owns Europe, you know.

1095
01:29:04,722 --> 01:29:06,119
He's smart.
We ought to think about Mexico.

1096
01:29:06,224 --> 01:29:07,225
To hell with Mexico, Jack.

1097
01:29:07,329 --> 01:29:10,203
No one airline should have
a monopoly on flying the Atlantic.

1098
01:29:10,307 --> 01:29:13,792
For Christ's sakes, it isn't fair. Look,
he owns Pan Am. He owns Congress.

1099
01:29:13,896 --> 01:29:17,878
He owns the Civil Aeronautics Board,
but he does not own the sky.

1100
01:29:17,982 --> 01:29:20,968
We are in a street fight with that
son of a bitch now, and I'm not gonna lose.

1101
01:29:21,072 --> 01:29:24,424
I've been fighting high-hat, Ivy-League
pricks like him my whole goddamn life.

1102
01:29:24,528 --> 01:29:28,302
And listen, fire Ray Loewy.
You goddamn heard me? Fire Ray Loewy.

1103
01:29:28,406 --> 01:29:31,726
He's spying for Trippe. That shit-heel
knew all about the goddamn buttons.

1104
01:29:31,831 --> 01:29:35,820
Spies in my midst, Jack.
Spies in my midst.

1105
01:29:50,841 --> 01:29:54,293
- Hello, Howard.
- Roland.

1106
01:29:55,638 --> 01:29:59,089
So, what can I do for you?

1107
01:30:02,186 --> 01:30:06,220
I want all the pictures you have
of Kate Hepburn and Spencer Tracy.

1108
01:30:06,324 --> 01:30:09,928
All the negatives.
And I want you to kill the story.

1109
01:30:10,032 --> 01:30:13,435
Howard, he's a married man.
He's a Catholic.

1110
01:30:13,539 --> 01:30:17,480
They're both movie stars.
Fair game all around.

1111
01:30:24,291 --> 01:30:27,366
My office knows where I am, Howard.

1112
01:30:27,471 --> 01:30:30,369
I'm not gonna kill you, Roland.

1113
01:30:30,474 --> 01:30:32,920
I don't do that.

1114
01:30:37,138 --> 01:30:39,925
- How much?
- Not for sale.

1115
01:30:42,685 --> 01:30:44,485
How much?

1116
01:30:45,487 --> 01:30:48,087
Not for sale.

1117
01:30:51,861 --> 01:30:55,768
You ever cheat on your wife, Roland?

1118
01:30:55,872 --> 01:30:58,895
You ever screw a coloured girl?

1119
01:30:58,999 --> 01:31:02,409
You ever steal anything?
You ever hurt anyone?

1120
01:31:04,331 --> 01:31:06,529
Good night, Howard.

1121
01:31:06,634 --> 01:31:10,955
You ever go to
a Communist Party meeting, Roland?

1122
01:31:19,388 --> 01:31:21,388
TWA stock.

1123
01:31:22,606 --> 01:31:24,406
How much?

1124
01:31:25,445 --> 01:31:29,136
- 50,000 shares.
- Ten.

1125
01:31:32,067 --> 01:31:34,067
All right.

1126
01:31:36,697 --> 01:31:38,434
This isn't gonna be that easy.

1127
01:31:38,539 --> 01:31:42,107
He's been making big contributions
on both sides of the aisle.

1128
01:31:42,211 --> 01:31:45,816
And Jack Frye is out there lobbying
everybody in town.

1129
01:31:45,920 --> 01:31:49,949
The French and British ambassadors
are all lined up on his side now.

1130
01:31:50,053 --> 01:31:54,078
I'm telling you, TWA is serious
about going international.

1131
01:31:54,182 --> 01:31:58,957
OK. Point, Mr Hughes.
Very well. You know what I think?

1132
01:31:59,061 --> 01:32:01,712
I think it's time for you to introduce
the Community Airline Bill

1133
01:32:01,816 --> 01:32:04,128
on the floor of the Senate.

1134
01:32:04,232 --> 01:32:07,004
- Is it done?
- My people are finishing it right now.

1135
01:32:07,109 --> 01:32:13,223
I also have to get you on the committee
investigating the national defence.

1136
01:32:14,193 --> 01:32:17,434
On the committee or chairman?

1137
01:32:17,538 --> 01:32:20,309
You know, I could be
much more effective as chairman.

1138
01:32:20,414 --> 01:32:24,186
It's a great public platform.
You know, it generates a lot of press.

1139
01:32:24,291 --> 01:32:26,234
Wasn't Truman chairman
of that committee?

1140
01:32:26,338 --> 01:32:30,276
Yeah, right. Now he's vice president.
Look what he did with it.

1141
01:32:30,380 --> 01:32:33,180
No, I think...

1142
01:32:33,305 --> 01:32:35,781
I think chairman.

1143
01:32:35,885 --> 01:32:39,244
What do you think?

1144
01:32:39,348 --> 01:32:42,123
- Chairman. That is interesting.
- Yeah.

1145
01:32:42,227 --> 01:32:45,712
Let me show you these specs
for the DC-4.

1146
01:32:45,816 --> 01:32:49,548
Ava, what do you think about
"Trans World Airlines"?

1147
01:32:49,652 --> 01:32:52,131
"Transcontinental and Western"
just doesn't fit any more.

1148
01:32:52,235 --> 01:32:55,011
Now that we're international,
we need a name that reflects that.

1149
01:32:55,115 --> 01:32:57,972
"Trans World" is good.
Kind of peppy.

1150
01:32:58,076 --> 01:33:00,276
TWA. Right?

1151
01:33:00,744 --> 01:33:03,690
Keep the same initials. That way you
don't need to repaint any of the planes.

1152
01:33:03,795 --> 01:33:06,980
That's you, always pinching pennies.

1153
01:33:07,084 --> 01:33:09,155
Hand me my wrap.

1154
01:33:23,137 --> 01:33:25,426
Knock it off.

1155
01:33:31,520 --> 01:33:35,095
I have something for you.

1156
01:33:35,199 --> 01:33:37,199
Stay here.

1157
01:33:42,531 --> 01:33:45,311
- What the hell is this?
- It's a present.

1158
01:33:45,415 --> 01:33:48,523
Go on and open it.

1159
01:33:48,627 --> 01:33:52,028
A box of trash.
You shouldn't have.

1160
01:33:52,133 --> 01:33:54,698
Keep looking.
Keep looking.

1161
01:33:58,672 --> 01:34:01,664
It's a Kashmiri sapphire.
Best in the world.

1162
01:34:01,768 --> 01:34:04,123
I had my boys all over the damn globe
looking for this.

1163
01:34:04,227 --> 01:34:06,999
- Why?
- Because.

1164
01:34:07,104 --> 01:34:10,960
Look.
It matches your eyes.

1165
01:34:11,064 --> 01:34:13,546
I am not for sale.

1166
01:34:13,650 --> 01:34:15,760
For Christ sakes, Ava,
it's just a present.

1167
01:34:15,865 --> 01:34:19,219
You can't buy me, Howard,
so stop trying.

1168
01:34:19,323 --> 01:34:22,848
Don't buy me any more diamonds or
sapphires or any other goddamn thing.

1169
01:34:22,952 --> 01:34:26,144
You can buy me dinner.
How about that?

1170
01:34:26,248 --> 01:34:27,570
Jesus, Ava.

1171
01:34:28,410 --> 01:34:31,607
It's bad enough I have to endure
those filthy gym shoes of yours,

1172
01:34:31,712 --> 01:34:36,695
but then I get all dolled up and we have
to go out in this old jalopy without a hood.

1173
01:34:36,799 --> 01:34:39,991
Ava, will you marry me?

1174
01:34:40,095 --> 01:34:41,410
No, Howard.

1175
01:34:41,514 --> 01:34:45,625
- Why not, for heaven's sake?
- In the first place, I don't love you.

1176
01:34:45,729 --> 01:34:48,167
In the second place, I'm still married.

1177
01:34:48,271 --> 01:34:53,048
Look, you got girls stashed all over town.
You got a damn harem just at the Bel-Air.

1178
01:34:53,152 --> 01:34:55,104
Why don't you marry
one of your bungalow girls?

1179
01:34:55,229 --> 01:34:57,512
Those are employees.
I'm not going to marry an employee.

1180
01:34:57,617 --> 01:35:00,519
Jesus Christ, how would that look?

1181
01:35:06,115 --> 01:35:08,606
Good Lord, what is going on?

1182
01:35:11,829 --> 01:35:14,534
- Oh, my God.
- Goddamn it.

1183
01:35:16,041 --> 01:35:17,241
Faith.

1184
01:35:18,093 --> 01:35:20,155
- Faith. What the hell?
- Look out.

1185
01:35:20,259 --> 01:35:23,208
Goddamn, what the hell are you doing?

1186
01:35:26,051 --> 01:35:28,915
- You OK, lady?
- What are you doing with her?

1187
01:35:29,019 --> 01:35:31,754
We're going to dinner, that's all.
Now get out of there.

1188
01:35:31,858 --> 01:35:33,919
Get that crazy bitch away from me.

1189
01:35:34,023 --> 01:35:37,425
- Don't you love me any more?
- Course I love you, pork chop.

1190
01:35:37,530 --> 01:35:39,758
- Ava, look over here.
- Howard.

1191
01:35:39,862 --> 01:35:42,099
- Hey.
- Come on, Howard.

1192
01:35:45,446 --> 01:35:47,750
Juan Trippe is working with
that goddamn Senator Brewster now.

1193
01:35:47,854 --> 01:35:48,682
They're after you, Howard.

1194
01:35:48,787 --> 01:35:51,940
If the Community Airline Bill becomes law,
we are finished, my friend.

1195
01:35:52,044 --> 01:35:54,340
Pan Am will have a legal monopoly
on international travel.

1196
01:35:54,444 --> 01:35:56,816
How can they justify it, Jack?
It's un-American.

1197
01:35:56,920 --> 01:35:59,262
Senator Brewster's saying that
nationalised foreign carriers,

1198
01:35:59,366 --> 01:36:00,738
like Air France and Lufthansa,

1199
01:36:00,842 --> 01:36:03,700
can offer lower rates
cos they don't have to compete, right?

1200
01:36:03,804 --> 01:36:05,977
So, hey, let's just get rid of
all that messy competition

1201
01:36:06,081 --> 01:36:08,959
and have a nationalised airline of our own,
and why don't we make it Pan Am?

1202
01:36:09,063 --> 01:36:10,836
I'm not kowtowing to Washington
or anyone else.

1203
01:36:10,940 --> 01:36:12,916
- Howard, I need you up here.
- One sec, Odie.

1204
01:36:13,020 --> 01:36:15,795
Look, we are Trans World Airlines,
all right?

1205
01:36:15,899 --> 01:36:20,465
Get me something with a circle or a globe
or something round, for God's sake.

1206
01:36:20,570 --> 01:36:23,306
Speak up. I know.
I don't give a rat's ass what he says.

1207
01:36:23,410 --> 01:36:26,309
I'm not making a single cut.
Tell him I'm releasing without a seal.

1208
01:36:26,413 --> 01:36:28,141
All right.
We gotta go public with this.

1209
01:36:28,245 --> 01:36:30,251
I'm gonna talk to Hearst,
see what press he can give me,

1210
01:36:30,355 --> 01:36:32,336
but sooner or later it's gonna come down
to a vote in the Senate,

1211
01:36:32,441 --> 01:36:33,567
so we gotta get senators on our side.

1212
01:36:33,671 --> 01:36:36,278
- What do you want me to do?
- Do what Trippe does, for Christ sake.

1213
01:36:36,382 --> 01:36:37,650
Set up some offshore operations.

1214
01:36:37,754 --> 01:36:40,011
See who's up for re-election.
Let's start making donations.

1215
01:36:40,115 --> 01:36:43,491
- So, you want me to bribe senators?
- I don't want them bribed, Jack.

1216
01:36:43,596 --> 01:36:46,201
I want it done legally.
I want them bought.

1217
01:36:46,306 --> 01:36:48,227
And put a team of investigators
on Senator Brewster.

1218
01:36:48,332 --> 01:36:50,833
I need to know everything there is to know
about that shit bag.

1219
01:36:50,937 --> 01:36:53,920
Where he goes, what he says
and who he screws.

1220
01:36:54,024 --> 01:36:57,218
- Get into it right now, Jack.
- You got it.

1221
01:37:03,732 --> 01:37:06,484
Just give me a second.

1222
01:37:07,403 --> 01:37:11,016
- All right. What do you need?
- Rudder and elevators.

1223
01:37:11,120 --> 01:37:15,937
These are fine. Have Simon and Pete get
back to me on the hydraulic assemblies.

1224
01:37:16,042 --> 01:37:19,815
- We need a secondary system here.
- OK.

1225
01:37:19,919 --> 01:37:22,779
And listen, we need to take
another look at the wheel.

1226
01:37:22,883 --> 01:37:27,367
- Jesus. The damn wheel?
- Yeah. It just doesn't feel right.

1227
01:37:27,471 --> 01:37:31,666
Christ almighty, you've seen 8,000
goddamn wheels. Choose one, please.

1228
01:37:31,770 --> 01:37:33,498
- Just one of them.
- I know. I know.

1229
01:37:33,602 --> 01:37:35,998
- This one?
- I know. This one...

1230
01:37:36,102 --> 01:37:38,213
This one's pretty close.

1231
01:37:38,317 --> 01:37:40,385
Pretty close.

1232
01:38:15,971 --> 01:38:16,971
Odie?

1233
01:38:18,312 --> 01:38:21,005
That man sweeping up over there.

1234
01:38:21,109 --> 01:38:24,259
Does he work for me?
I mean, have you seen him before?

1235
01:38:24,363 --> 01:38:27,106
Name's Nick.
Something like that.

1236
01:38:29,151 --> 01:38:31,025
Why's he looking at me?

1237
01:38:33,155 --> 01:38:35,231
I don't know.

1238
01:38:36,492 --> 01:38:39,899
Fire him. And make sure they use
damp brooms from now on.

1239
01:38:40,004 --> 01:38:43,403
Respiratory diseases are expensive, and
I don't want a bunch of damn lawsuits.

1240
01:38:43,507 --> 01:38:46,366
OK. But can we at least proceed
with the instrument panel we discussed?

1241
01:38:46,471 --> 01:38:47,534
The tool shop's ready to go.

1242
01:38:47,638 --> 01:38:49,557
- No. I wanna see the blueprints again.
- Howard,

1243
01:38:49,661 --> 01:38:52,161
the deadline is now completely unrealistic.

1244
01:38:52,265 --> 01:38:54,955
At this rate, the war is gonna be over
by the time she's done.

1245
01:38:55,059 --> 01:38:57,499
Now, I need you here
to help consult on vital decisions

1246
01:38:57,604 --> 01:38:59,295
and you're off dealing with movies.

1247
01:38:59,399 --> 01:39:02,487
You got a thousand goddamn workers
waiting for you to make a decision here...

1248
01:39:02,591 --> 01:39:04,175
Hey. Odie.

1249
01:39:04,279 --> 01:39:07,761
Take it easy, all right?
I understand you're under a lot of pressure,

1250
01:39:07,865 --> 01:39:13,095
but it's gonna do me no good if you
crack up on me like that. All right? Look.

1251
01:39:13,199 --> 01:39:15,515
Take a couple of hours off, all right?

1252
01:39:15,620 --> 01:39:17,562
- You relax a little.
- OK.

1253
01:39:17,666 --> 01:39:20,272
- See your wife.
- OK.

1254
01:39:20,376 --> 01:39:22,376
All right.

1255
01:39:22,627 --> 01:39:26,405
- Be sure to show me all the blueprints.
- All right.

1256
01:39:27,460 --> 01:39:29,951
Show me all the blueprints.

1257
01:39:30,171 --> 01:39:32,240
Show me all the blueprints.

1258
01:39:32,345 --> 01:39:37,411
I'm serious, now.
Show me all the blueprints.

1259
01:39:37,515 --> 01:39:39,524
Show me all the blue...
Show me all the blueprints.

1260
01:39:39,628 --> 01:39:42,293
- Howard...
- Show me all the blueprints.

1261
01:39:42,397 --> 01:39:44,550
Show me all the blueprints.
Show me all the blueprints.

1262
01:39:44,654 --> 01:39:48,425
Show me all the blueprints.
Show me all the blueprints.

1263
01:39:48,530 --> 01:39:51,264
I want to get this done right,
so show me all the blueprints.

1264
01:39:51,368 --> 01:39:52,929
Show me all the blueprints.

1265
01:39:53,033 --> 01:39:55,060
Show me all the blueprints.
Show me all the blueprints.

1266
01:39:55,164 --> 01:39:57,563
Show me all the blueprints.
Show me all the blueprints.

1267
01:39:57,667 --> 01:40:00,059
- Show me all the blueprints.
- Howard.

1268
01:40:00,163 --> 01:40:03,575
Show me all the blueprints.

1269
01:40:03,788 --> 01:40:07,737
Show me all the blueprints.

1270
01:40:07,958 --> 01:40:11,078
Show me all the blueprints.

1271
01:40:32,233 --> 01:40:34,433
Quarantine.

1272
01:40:35,328 --> 01:40:36,128
Q...

1273
01:40:37,410 --> 01:40:38,210
U...

1274
01:40:39,662 --> 01:40:40,462
A...

1275
01:40:42,502 --> 01:40:43,302
R...

1276
01:40:45,211 --> 01:40:46,011
A...

1277
01:40:47,295 --> 01:40:48,095
N...

1278
01:40:49,092 --> 01:40:49,892
T...

1279
01:40:51,507 --> 01:40:52,307
I...

1280
01:40:53,343 --> 01:40:54,143
N...

1281
01:40:59,760 --> 01:41:00,160
E.

1282
01:41:04,598 --> 01:41:06,674
Quarantine.

1283
01:41:44,680 --> 01:41:47,877
General McEwan. Colonel Bertrang.
Thanks for coming down.

1284
01:41:47,981 --> 01:41:53,432
- Odie, you reading me OK?
- Yeah, Howard. You're A-OK.

1285
01:41:57,234 --> 01:42:02,655
All right, flight controls are active.
She's all yours.

1286
01:42:09,288 --> 01:42:11,697
She's spotless, Odie.
No wiggle on the wheel or throttles.

1287
01:42:11,801 --> 01:42:13,867
Take it easy.

1288
01:42:30,518 --> 01:42:34,341
- How does she sound, Howard?
- She's whispering to me, buddy.

1289
01:42:34,445 --> 01:42:37,225
All right, make her sing.

1290
01:42:57,086 --> 01:43:00,532
Well, Odie, she can fly.
Congratulations.

1291
01:43:00,636 --> 01:43:05,831
I'm glad to hear it. Retract landing gear and
climb to 5,000 feet on a heading of 2-7-0.

1292
01:43:05,935 --> 01:43:12,381
Retracting landing gear and climbing
to 5,000 feet on a heading 2-7-0.

1293
01:43:16,397 --> 01:43:18,677
- Jesus, she's fast.
- What's your airspeed, Howard?

1294
01:43:18,781 --> 01:43:22,553
- 292.
- All right, take her back to 200.

1295
01:43:22,657 --> 01:43:24,278
No damn way.

1296
01:43:49,930 --> 01:43:51,751
All right, Howard, we gotta bring her home.

1297
01:43:51,855 --> 01:43:55,173
Scheduled time of one hour and 45
minutes has elapsed. Set course for 0-9-0.

1298
01:43:55,277 --> 01:44:00,341
- Give me ten more minutes. Roger that.
- Negative. Howard, bring her home.

1299
01:44:00,445 --> 01:44:01,634
OK. OK.

1300
01:44:01,738 --> 01:44:06,684
Setting course for 0-9-0.

1301
01:44:06,788 --> 01:44:08,939
Preparing to descend.

1302
01:44:12,411 --> 01:44:15,910
- Jesus.
- What is it, Howard?

1303
01:44:16,957 --> 01:44:20,614
The right wing just dipped.
I'm losing starboard engine.

1304
01:44:20,718 --> 01:44:23,658
Increasing power to 2800 rpm.

1305
01:44:23,762 --> 01:44:25,410
Cutting back.

1306
01:44:25,514 --> 01:44:27,714
Increasing starboard engine only.

1307
01:44:28,803 --> 01:44:31,960
Cutting back.
I'm losing altitude.

1308
01:44:32,064 --> 01:44:35,585
Howard, check starboard engine
manifold pressure.

1309
01:44:35,690 --> 01:44:39,009
- It's good, but rpms are low.
- Are both starboard props turning?

1310
01:44:39,113 --> 01:44:40,713
Hold on.

1311
01:44:40,907 --> 01:44:43,928
It looks like they are, but she's pulling me
back and starboard, Odie.

1312
01:44:44,032 --> 01:44:47,721
- How bad is your cross control?
- I'm at full left rudder and full left aileron,

1313
01:44:47,826 --> 01:44:50,269
but she won't stay level.
Goddamn it.

1314
01:44:50,373 --> 01:44:53,357
- Howard, give us your position.
- 2,000 feet over...

1315
01:44:53,462 --> 01:44:57,525
Oh, Christ, I don't know.
Beverly Hills. 1,500 feet.

1316
01:44:57,629 --> 01:44:59,450
We're going down.

1317
01:45:00,334 --> 01:45:03,947
I'm gonna try for the Wilshire Country
Club. Ninth hole. You reading me?

1318
01:45:04,051 --> 01:45:08,091
Wilshire Country Club. Copy that.
Howard, reduce engines to one thousand.

1319
01:45:08,717 --> 01:45:11,165
I'm going down.

1320
01:45:11,269 --> 01:45:13,753
I'm not gonna make it, buddy.

1321
01:45:19,979 --> 01:45:21,141
Goddamn it.

1322
01:47:24,353 --> 01:47:27,844
Is there anyone else inside?
Is there anyone else?

1323
01:47:27,948 --> 01:47:28,548
No.

1324
01:47:33,446 --> 01:47:36,392
I'm Howard Hughes, the aviator.

1325
01:47:36,496 --> 01:47:38,096
Come on.

1326
01:47:45,791 --> 01:47:49,032
Every inch of wiring and fluid connection...

1327
01:47:49,136 --> 01:47:51,825
He has burns to 78 per cent of his body.

1328
01:47:51,930 --> 01:47:54,997
Nine ribs are shattered.
Not broken, shattered.

1329
01:47:55,102 --> 01:47:59,626
As are his nose, his chin, his cheek,
his left knee, his left elbow.

1330
01:47:59,730 --> 01:48:02,835
He has 60 lacerations on his face
to the bone.

1331
01:48:02,940 --> 01:48:05,797
His chest was crushed,
so his left lung has collapsed

1332
01:48:05,901 --> 01:48:10,346
and his heart has shifted entirely
to the right side of his chest cavity.

1333
01:48:10,450 --> 01:48:12,344
Jesus, God.

1334
01:48:12,448 --> 01:48:15,806
He's getting blood transfusions now, but...

1335
01:48:15,910 --> 01:48:19,396
- Whose blood?
- I'm sorry?

1336
01:48:19,500 --> 01:48:22,816
- Whose blood?
- From our stock.

1337
01:48:22,920 --> 01:48:24,776
He's not gonna like that.

1338
01:48:24,880 --> 01:48:30,571
Mr Dietrich, I doubt he's ever
gonna like or dislike anything again.

1339
01:48:30,676 --> 01:48:33,042
I'm terribly sorry.

1340
01:49:01,281 --> 01:49:03,268
Orange juice.

1341
01:49:03,372 --> 01:49:04,772
It's...

1342
01:49:06,212 --> 01:49:09,453
It's not fresh from the kitchen, so I...

1343
01:49:10,499 --> 01:49:14,627
I have them make it here so I can see.

1344
01:49:19,716 --> 01:49:21,792
Look at me.

1345
01:49:24,304 --> 01:49:28,000
I'm a monster.
Yeah.

1346
01:49:28,104 --> 01:49:31,800
Orange juice has nutritional value.

1347
01:49:32,854 --> 01:49:38,183
There's some flies
outside my window, though.

1348
01:49:38,287 --> 01:49:39,287
So...

1349
01:49:42,072 --> 01:49:44,148
little Howard likes...

1350
01:49:46,076 --> 01:49:47,476
citrus.

1351
01:49:49,127 --> 01:49:51,197
Don't he just?

1352
01:49:54,126 --> 01:49:55,726
Tell me.

1353
01:49:57,471 --> 01:50:01,325
An oil seal ripped off
the starboard rear propeller.

1354
01:50:01,429 --> 01:50:05,332
When the pressure dropped,
the prop reversed pitch.

1355
01:50:05,436 --> 01:50:07,627
Do you understand me?

1356
01:50:07,731 --> 01:50:12,712
Howard, I'm sorry to have to tell you
this now, but there's something else.

1357
01:50:12,816 --> 01:50:15,267
- You following me?
- Yeah.

1358
01:50:17,065 --> 01:50:22,892
The Air Force cancelled
the contract on the Hercules.

1359
01:50:22,996 --> 01:50:28,687
The war's over now,
and they say they don't need it any more.

1360
01:50:28,792 --> 01:50:33,403
I have to know what you want me to do.
Should I release the staff?

1361
01:50:33,507 --> 01:50:35,507
How far...

1362
01:50:36,005 --> 01:50:38,955
from finishing?

1363
01:50:40,130 --> 01:50:42,871
- About six months.
- No, no.

1364
01:50:42,976 --> 01:50:44,776
In money.

1365
01:50:48,222 --> 01:50:52,302
Seven million.
Maybe more.

1366
01:50:58,023 --> 01:50:59,823
Build it.

1367
01:51:00,952 --> 01:51:03,952
Build it, Odie.

1368
01:51:04,787 --> 01:51:06,187
Howard,

1369
01:51:06,915 --> 01:51:10,941
a Constellation crashed
outside Reading, Pennsylvania.

1370
01:51:11,045 --> 01:51:14,903
The Civil Aeronautics Board
has grounded the whole fleet.

1371
01:51:24,841 --> 01:51:30,927
You know, Juan Trippe sent me flowers.

1372
01:51:33,016 --> 01:51:35,092
Take a look.

1373
01:51:38,605 --> 01:51:43,511
- What did you do with all the others?
- I had them taken out.

1374
01:51:43,615 --> 01:51:46,813
They attract aphids.

1375
01:51:47,823 --> 01:51:53,233
Aphids are...
They're just awful little creatures.

1376
01:51:53,338 --> 01:51:54,538
But...

1377
01:51:57,416 --> 01:51:59,492
But these ones...

1378
01:52:00,669 --> 01:52:04,797
I wanted to see these ones every day.

1379
01:52:11,889 --> 01:52:14,963
Can white elephants really fly?
That's everybody's question.

1380
01:52:15,068 --> 01:52:17,838
It's the hull of the world's
mightiest airplane,

1381
01:52:17,942 --> 01:52:20,300
a flying boat built
by Howard Hughes.

1382
01:52:20,404 --> 01:52:24,553
220 feet long, it towers higher
than a five-storey building.

1383
01:52:24,658 --> 01:52:28,724
Power lines have to be cut as it starts
the trip from Culver City, California,

1384
01:52:28,828 --> 01:52:30,975
to the Pacific, 30 miles away.

1385
01:52:31,080 --> 01:52:34,399
Moving the 60-ton load
is quite an engineering problem.

1386
01:52:34,503 --> 01:52:38,440
But you gotta ask, "Was anything this big
ever supposed to fly?"

1387
01:52:38,544 --> 01:52:43,074
There goes one wing section.
160 feet long with four engine housings.

1388
01:52:43,178 --> 01:52:46,577
Double that, and you've got
some idea of the wing spread.

1389
01:52:46,682 --> 01:52:50,837
An airplane that challenges
a mountain for sheer size.

1390
01:52:57,601 --> 01:52:59,921
How long can they keep us grounded?

1391
01:53:00,025 --> 01:53:03,428
Till they finish investigating
the Reading crash. Could be months.

1392
01:53:03,532 --> 01:53:08,179
Jesus. You're already running a
$14 million deficit. How you gonna afford...

1393
01:53:08,283 --> 01:53:10,598
We go international,
we'll make it up.

1394
01:53:10,703 --> 01:53:13,356
Look, Brewster's CAB bill
just isn't going away.

1395
01:53:13,461 --> 01:53:16,046
That bill passes and you bought
all these goddamn planes for nothing.

1396
01:53:16,151 --> 01:53:17,444
- We're fighting the CAB bill.
- Yeah.

1397
01:53:17,548 --> 01:53:19,858
Meanwhile, how do you suggest
we keep TWA flying?

1398
01:53:19,962 --> 01:53:21,844
And don't say, "Just go to Toolco,"
this time.

1399
01:53:21,948 --> 01:53:24,388
As it is we're pumping every damn cent
we have into the Hercules,

1400
01:53:24,492 --> 01:53:27,615
which, I might add,
the Air Force doesn't even want any more.

1401
01:53:27,720 --> 01:53:30,540
Now look, Howard, I'm glad Jack here
is feeling so sunny about things,

1402
01:53:30,644 --> 01:53:31,562
but I've seen the books.

1403
01:53:31,666 --> 01:53:34,461
And I'm telling it to you straight,
I'm not just crying wolf this time,

1404
01:53:34,565 --> 01:53:37,587
we're in serious trouble.
You've got to make a choice.

1405
01:53:37,691 --> 01:53:43,057
You want to be bankrupted by
the big plane or by the big airline?

1406
01:53:49,987 --> 01:53:53,727
Go see Thomas Parkinson
at the Equitable in New York.

1407
01:53:53,831 --> 01:53:58,940
Get a loan against all the TWA equipment
and capital. Use the planes as collateral.

1408
01:53:59,045 --> 01:54:03,489
Hell, use the desks, use the pens,
use everything we got.

1409
01:54:03,593 --> 01:54:08,323
- Try to get me 40 million.
- And if TWA defaults on the loan?

1410
01:54:08,427 --> 01:54:11,542
Well, then Juan Trippe buys us cheap.

1411
01:54:13,844 --> 01:54:16,919
Under my bed. You put
a goddamn microphone under my bed.

1412
01:54:17,023 --> 01:54:19,250
Honey, listen to me.
I am concerned about you, baby.

1413
01:54:19,355 --> 01:54:22,879
- I just want to make sure you're OK.
- And who is in that car?

1414
01:54:22,983 --> 01:54:25,380
That goddamn car
has been with me 24 hours a day.

1415
01:54:25,485 --> 01:54:27,383
That car is there for your protection.

1416
01:54:27,488 --> 01:54:30,929
The only one I need protection from is you,
you sick bastard.

1417
01:54:31,033 --> 01:54:32,681
You don't own me, Howard.

1418
01:54:32,786 --> 01:54:35,937
I'm not one of your teenage whores,
and I'm not some damn airplane.

1419
01:54:36,041 --> 01:54:38,108
Now look, honey,
I'll have them take all the bugs out.

1420
01:54:38,212 --> 01:54:40,485
You just have to understand
that I need to know where you are.

1421
01:54:40,589 --> 01:54:43,195
- Why?
- Because I worry about you, that's why.

1422
01:54:43,299 --> 01:54:45,099
Bullshit.

1423
01:54:47,920 --> 01:54:51,086
What do you mean, all the bugs?

1424
01:54:56,011 --> 01:54:58,716
What do you mean, all the bugs?

1425
01:55:00,933 --> 01:55:02,964
There's more.

1426
01:55:03,068 --> 01:55:04,868
How many?

1427
01:55:05,279 --> 01:55:07,340
I don't know, 12.
12, maybe.

1428
01:55:07,444 --> 01:55:11,844
And on the telephones.

1429
01:55:11,995 --> 01:55:15,681
Oh, Christ, Howard, on the telephone?

1430
01:55:15,785 --> 01:55:17,434
You listen to my phone calls?

1431
01:55:17,539 --> 01:55:21,395
No, no, no, honey. I would never do that.
I'd never do that. I...

1432
01:55:21,499 --> 01:55:24,662
I just read the transcripts, that's all.

1433
01:55:29,044 --> 01:55:31,324
What do you want to know, Howard?

1434
01:55:31,429 --> 01:55:33,658
Was I screwing Artie Shaw last night?

1435
01:55:33,762 --> 01:55:36,537
Was I screwing Sinatra the night before?
You bet.

1436
01:55:36,641 --> 01:55:39,294
Everyone told me you were
a goddamn lunatic but I didn't listen.

1437
01:55:39,398 --> 01:55:42,379
It's no wonder Kate Hepburn
dumped your demented ass.

1438
01:55:42,483 --> 01:55:44,431
Shut your goddamn mouth.

1439
01:55:49,189 --> 01:55:52,309
Get out, you pathetic freak.

1440
01:55:59,950 --> 01:56:01,550
Get out.

1441
01:56:02,085 --> 01:56:04,025
Is everything all right, sir?

1442
01:56:04,129 --> 01:56:07,324
Take out all the bugs.

1443
01:56:08,500 --> 01:56:10,781
Except for the one on the bedroom phone.

1444
01:56:10,885 --> 01:56:13,962
Sir, the FBI are at the house.

1445
01:56:14,965 --> 01:56:17,926
This is outrageous. Everything in these
offices is the private property

1446
01:56:18,051 --> 01:56:21,043
of Hughes Productions.
My legal counsel is on the way...

1447
01:56:21,147 --> 01:56:25,088
Federal warrant.
Don't interfere with this search, sir.

1448
01:56:44,244 --> 01:56:47,568
Dateline Los Angeles.
Howard Hughes has a new house guest.

1449
01:56:47,673 --> 01:56:51,443
No, it's not another beautiful starlet
this time. It's the FBI.

1450
01:56:51,547 --> 01:56:53,158
Noah, you have got to help, all right?

1451
01:56:53,263 --> 01:56:55,573
This is the tenth goddamn time
they have been here.

1452
01:56:55,678 --> 01:56:59,163
Rumour has it that federal agents working
for Senator Ralph Owen Brewster

1453
01:56:59,268 --> 01:57:03,166
have practically taken up residence
in the aviation mogul's Hollywood home.

1454
01:57:03,271 --> 01:57:07,964
Look, they are touching things.
Noah, Noah, they are touching things.

1455
01:57:08,068 --> 01:57:12,094
Just keep yourself calm and I'll be
down there as soon as I can, Howard.

1456
01:57:12,198 --> 01:57:15,059
Howard?
Howard?

1457
01:57:40,300 --> 01:57:42,331
Howard, hello.

1458
01:57:42,436 --> 01:57:45,790
- Owen, nice to see you again.
- Good to see you. Come on inside.

1459
01:57:45,894 --> 01:57:48,503
- Emma, you can start the lunch now.
- Yes, sir.

1460
01:57:48,608 --> 01:57:53,173
Well, really lovely room.
Nicely decorated.

1461
01:57:53,277 --> 01:57:55,136
Thank you.
Here, have a seat.

1462
01:57:55,240 --> 01:57:56,608
- Thanks.
- Thanks for coming by.

1463
01:57:56,733 --> 01:57:59,763
I just thought you and I should have
a chance to talk privately.

1464
01:57:59,868 --> 01:58:02,892
You know, outside the office.

1465
01:58:02,996 --> 01:58:04,396
Well...

1466
01:58:05,753 --> 01:58:07,782
I appreciate that, Owen.

1467
01:58:15,085 --> 01:58:20,746
So, you're coming out pretty strong
against the CAB bill.

1468
01:58:20,850 --> 01:58:26,124
- You're coming on pretty strong for it.
- Well, it's my bill, Howard. You know?

1469
01:58:26,228 --> 01:58:28,919
Look, I believe sincerely that

1470
01:58:29,023 --> 01:58:33,310
America cannot afford to have
more than one international carrier.

1471
01:58:34,980 --> 01:58:39,969
So, do you think it's fair that one airline
should have a monopoly on...

1472
01:58:40,074 --> 01:58:43,681
A monopoly? No, no, no.
No, no, no. Oh, no.

1473
01:58:43,786 --> 01:58:49,313
No, I think one airline could do it better,
see, without competition.

1474
01:58:49,417 --> 01:58:54,195
All I'm thinking about are the interests,
the needs of the American passenger.

1475
01:58:54,299 --> 01:58:57,292
That's just beautiful.
What is that?

1476
01:59:01,506 --> 01:59:03,827
What is that?
Is that a...

1477
01:59:03,931 --> 01:59:06,917
Is that a yak?
Some kind of a yak?

1478
01:59:07,021 --> 01:59:11,130
No, that's a llama. My wife picked that up
when we were in Peru a year ago.

1479
01:59:11,234 --> 01:59:14,640
Son of a gun.
A real llama.

1480
01:59:17,105 --> 01:59:20,014
- From Peru?
- Yeah. A year ago.

1481
01:59:20,118 --> 01:59:22,556
Yeah, it was about a year ago.

1482
01:59:22,660 --> 01:59:24,889
- Lunch is served, Senator.
- Good. OK.

1483
01:59:24,994 --> 01:59:27,610
Come on, let's go have some lunch.

1484
01:59:28,659 --> 01:59:32,604
Now did you...
Did you actually get to see any llamas?

1485
01:59:32,709 --> 01:59:34,652
No, no.
My wife just liked the painting.

1486
01:59:34,756 --> 01:59:38,527
It's a hell of an interesting animal.
I'll have to read up on those.

1487
01:59:38,631 --> 01:59:43,366
How do you spell that?
Like Fernando Lamas?

1488
01:59:43,470 --> 01:59:45,868
No, no, no. It's...

1489
01:59:45,972 --> 01:59:49,171
It's the animal. It's got two Ls.
Here, come on, have a seat.

1490
01:59:59,147 --> 02:00:02,516
It's brook trout.
Hope you like fish.

1491
02:00:04,152 --> 02:00:05,973
I love it.

1492
02:00:06,077 --> 02:00:07,477
Thanks.

1493
02:00:22,963 --> 02:00:27,126
I know you're not a drinking man,
so I hope water's OK.

1494
02:00:39,897 --> 02:00:41,297
Thanks.

1495
02:00:47,154 --> 02:00:51,560
All right, let's get down to business.
Let's talk turkey.

1496
02:00:51,665 --> 02:00:54,772
My investigators...

1497
02:00:54,876 --> 02:00:58,111
My investigators
have turned up a lot of dirt.

1498
02:00:58,215 --> 02:01:02,069
Could be really embarrassing
if this stuff got out.

1499
02:01:02,173 --> 02:01:05,864
I'd like to save you
from that embarrassment.

1500
02:01:05,968 --> 02:01:08,206
That's very kind of you, Owen.

1501
02:01:11,136 --> 02:01:14,968
My committee has the power
to hold public hearings.

1502
02:01:16,516 --> 02:01:19,047
I'd like to spare you that.

1503
02:01:19,151 --> 02:01:21,591
Would you, now?

1504
02:01:21,696 --> 02:01:22,696
Look.

1505
02:01:23,324 --> 02:01:28,099
Do you want to go down in history
as a war profiteer, Howard?

1506
02:01:28,203 --> 02:01:30,816
Is that what you want?

1507
02:01:31,990 --> 02:01:34,660
What do you want, Owen?

1508
02:01:35,786 --> 02:01:42,030
You agree to support my CAB bill
and I won't hold public hearings.

1509
02:01:42,134 --> 02:01:43,779
- I can't do that.
- Why not?

1510
02:01:43,883 --> 02:01:47,701
I can't do that, Owen.
The CAB would kill TWA.

1511
02:01:47,805 --> 02:01:51,411
Sell TWA to Pan Am.

1512
02:01:51,515 --> 02:01:55,666
You'll get a good price.
You'll get a fair price, I'm telling you.

1513
02:01:55,770 --> 02:01:57,962
And then?
Then you won't go public?

1514
02:01:58,066 --> 02:02:03,047
Right. That's right. The investigation's
closed. Nobody knows a thing.

1515
02:02:03,152 --> 02:02:06,515
It's better for everybody.

1516
02:02:10,779 --> 02:02:12,727
You know, Owen, I'm...

1517
02:02:12,831 --> 02:02:14,783
- I'm still wondering one thing.
- What's that?

1518
02:02:14,908 --> 02:02:18,271
You know the picture of the llama
you got last year?

1519
02:02:18,375 --> 02:02:21,122
- Yeah.
- Where'd you sail from?

1520
02:02:21,874 --> 02:02:23,866
We didn't sail.
We flew.

1521
02:02:25,127 --> 02:02:27,534
- You flew?
- Yeah.

1522
02:02:35,137 --> 02:02:38,999
Are you sure you want to do this, Owen?

1523
02:02:39,103 --> 02:02:41,637
You want to go to war with me?

1524
02:02:46,356 --> 02:02:51,511
It isn't me, Howard.
It's the United States government.

1525
02:02:51,616 --> 02:02:54,725
We just beat Germany and Japan.

1526
02:02:54,829 --> 02:02:57,030
Who the hell are you?

1527
02:03:02,206 --> 02:03:07,322
You tell Juan Trippe something for me,
all right?

1528
02:03:07,426 --> 02:03:10,366
Tell him thanks for the flowers.

1529
02:03:10,470 --> 02:03:15,885
And he can kiss both sides of my ass.

1530
02:03:39,785 --> 02:03:42,271
Well, we have a long list of particulars.

1531
02:03:42,375 --> 02:03:46,777
Chief among them is that
he defrauded the American government

1532
02:03:46,881 --> 02:03:50,450
of $56 million while we were at war,
when we could least afford it.

1533
02:03:50,554 --> 02:03:53,620
While brave men were dying
on the beaches of Normandy,

1534
02:03:53,725 --> 02:03:56,623
Mr Hughes was picking the pocket
of the American taxpayer.

1535
02:03:56,728 --> 02:03:58,128
I sleep

1536
02:03:59,564 --> 02:04:04,564
in this room in the dark.

1537
02:04:05,026 --> 02:04:08,048
I'll have him dragged here
to Washington if I have to.

1538
02:04:08,153 --> 02:04:10,383
I want to see the whites of his lies.

1539
02:04:10,487 --> 02:04:14,972
I have a place where I sleep.

1540
02:04:15,076 --> 02:04:18,184
- He has a lot of questions to answer.
- I have a chair.

1541
02:04:18,288 --> 02:04:23,231
Particularly about that
monstrous boondoggle of his.

1542
02:04:23,336 --> 02:04:28,112
That model airplane he's building.
You know, that flying lumberyard.

1543
02:04:28,217 --> 02:04:31,366
That Spruce Goose.

1544
02:04:31,470 --> 02:04:34,213
No, we'll get him here.

1545
02:04:37,259 --> 02:04:41,166
That's just beautiful.

1546
02:04:41,270 --> 02:04:43,505
I like the desert.

1547
02:04:45,100 --> 02:04:50,723
It's hot there in the desert, but it's clean.
It's clean.

1548
02:04:52,858 --> 02:04:55,693
I need to sleep.

1549
02:04:57,946 --> 02:05:01,362
I should drink something first.

1550
02:05:04,578 --> 02:05:06,903
I should drink something first.

1551
02:05:11,126 --> 02:05:13,926
Wait a minute.

1552
02:05:14,138 --> 02:05:16,870
What if that milk is sour?

1553
02:05:16,975 --> 02:05:19,285
If that milk is bad,

1554
02:05:19,390 --> 02:05:24,751
I shouldn't pick up the bottle of milk
with my right hand,

1555
02:05:24,855 --> 02:05:30,684
and I shouldn't take the top off
with my left hand.

1556
02:05:32,022 --> 02:05:35,263
Put it in my pocket.

1557
